/* Domain 16 — Windows, Windows Server & Active Directory Administration (facts as of October 2026) */
BOOTCAMP.phases.push({
id:"p16", num:16, title:"Windows, Windows Server & Active Directory Administration", short:"Windows",
tagline:"Understand the NT kernel, drive Windows with PowerShell, build and secure a Windows Server domain, manage endpoints from the cloud, and read Active Directory the way both administrators and attackers must.",
prereq:"Domains 1 (hardware: privilege rings, virtual memory), 2 (networking: DNS, DHCP, ports), 3 (Linux: processes, services, shells) and 7 (cybersecurity: authentication, least privilege). Every lab runs on your own machines or in an evaluation tenant you created.",
outcomes:[
"Explain the NT architecture — user mode versus kernel mode, the executive, processes, threads, handles, access tokens, services, the registry and NTFS — and inspect all of it with Sysinternals.",
"Use PowerShell as an object pipeline: discover cmdlets, write functions and modules with error handling, remote into machines over WinRM, schedule tasks and automate audits.",
"Choose Windows Server editions and installation options, deploy DNS, DHCP, file, print and Hyper-V roles, manage them with Windows Admin Center, and do core-based licensing arithmetic.",
"Design and build an Active Directory domain: forests, domains, trusts, OUs, groups, Kerberos and NTLM, Group Policy precedence, FSMO roles and replication.",
"Manage identity and endpoints in the cloud: Microsoft Entra ID, hybrid identity, Intune, Windows Autopilot, Conditional Access, MFA and Windows Hello for Business — the MD-102 skill set.",
"Harden, patch and troubleshoot Windows: audit policy and Event Viewer, Defender, BitLocker, Windows LAPS, the firewall, WSUS and Windows Update for Business, backup, and performance counters.",
"Describe the tiered administration model and the common Active Directory weaknesses conceptually, and detect misuse with Sysmon and Windows auditing — defensively, on systems you own.",
"Prepare for the MD-102 and AZ-802 exams with a study order and a lab that mirrors their objectives."
],
texts:["Windows Internals, 7th edition, Parts 1 and 2 (Yosifovich, Ionescu, Russinovich, Solomon; Allievi et al., Microsoft Press)","Learn PowerShell in a Month of Lunches, 4th edition (Plunk, Petty, Leonhardt, Jones, Hicks, Manning 2022)","Microsoft Learn: Windows Server documentation and the AD DS design and planning guides","Microsoft Learn: the MD-102 and AZ-802 study guides (skills measured, updated 2026)"],
modules:[
{id:"p16m1", title:"Windows Architecture: The NT Kernel, Processes, the Registry and NTFS", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Separate user mode from kernel mode and name the executive components that live in ntoskrnl.exe.","Define process, thread, handle and access token precisely and read them in Process Explorer.","Explain how services, the Service Control Manager and service accounts work.","Navigate the registry hives on disk and in memory and know the persistence keys that matter.","Read an NTFS security descriptor and predict effective access; use Autoruns and Procmon for triage."],
body:`<p>What is actually running when you see a Windows desktop? Not "Windows" — a few hundred processes, each a container of virtual memory, threads and handles, all asking one privileged program for service. Every troubleshooting session, every malware triage in Domain 11 and every privilege-escalation finding in a pentest report is a story about these objects. You know the vocabulary from Domain 1 (privilege rings, virtual memory) and Domain 3 (Linux processes and daemons); this module gives you the Windows names and the Windows tools.</p>
<h4>Two modes, one kernel</h4>
<p>Code runs in <strong>user mode</strong> (ring 3: applications, subsystem DLLs, services) or <strong>kernel mode</strong> (ring 0: the kernel, the executive, drivers). <code>ntoskrnl.exe</code> holds both the <em>kernel</em> proper (scheduling, interrupts, synchronization) and the <strong>executive</strong>: the Object Manager (every process, file, registry key and token is an <em>object</em> with a security descriptor), the Process and Memory Managers (page tables and paging — Domain 1 again), the I/O Manager (driver stacks), the Security Reference Monitor (every access check), the Configuration Manager (the registry) and the Cache, Plug and Play and Power managers. Graphics live in <code>win32k.sys</code>; the Win32 subsystem process <code>csrss.exe</code> runs in user mode, which makes Windows a hybrid kernel. A program never calls the kernel directly: <code>kernel32.dll</code> calls <code>ntdll.dll</code>, which executes the <code>syscall</code> instruction. That boundary is why an application crash kills one process while a buggy driver kills the machine with a bugcheck (blue screen): drivers share the kernel's address space.</p>
<h4>Processes, threads, handles, tokens</h4>
<p>A <em>process</em> is a container: a private virtual address space (128 TB of user space on x64), a <strong>handle</strong> table, an access token and at least one thread. A <em>thread</em> is what the scheduler runs, at a priority from 0 to 31. A <strong>handle</strong> is an index into the handle table referring to a kernel object; values are multiples of 4, so <code>0x1C4</code> is entry 113, and a count that climbs forever is a leak. The <strong>access token</strong> answers "who is this?": the user's <strong>security identifier</strong> (SID), group SIDs, privileges such as SeDebugPrivilege and an integrity level (Low, Medium, High, System). Memorize a few SIDs: <code>S-1-5-18</code> is LocalSystem; <code>S-1-5-21-&lt;domain&gt;-500</code> is the built-in Administrator, 512 Domain Admins, 519 Enterprise Admins. User Account Control is a token trick: an administrator's logon yields a filtered Medium token for daily work and a full High token that the consent prompt hands to elevated programs.</p>
<p>Boot order and the critical processes: UEFI → <code>bootmgfw.efi</code> → <code>winload.efi</code> → <code>ntoskrnl.exe</code> → <code>smss.exe</code> → <code>csrss.exe</code> and <code>wininit.exe</code> in Session 0, which start <code>services.exe</code> and <code>lsass.exe</code>; <code>winlogon.exe</code> in your session runs <code>userinit.exe</code>, then <code>explorer.exe</code>. Services live in Session 0, isolated from user desktops. <strong>LSASS</strong> performs logons and holds credential material for single sign-on, which is why Module 7 spends so long protecting it.</p>
<h4>Services</h4>
<p><code>services.exe</code> is the Service Control Manager; most Microsoft services are DLLs hosted by <code>svchost.exe</code>. Configuration lives under <code>HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\&lt;name&gt;</code>: image path, start type (Boot, System, Automatic, Automatic Delayed, Manual, Disabled) and account. The account sets the blast radius of a compromise:</p>
<table><tr><th>Account</th><th>Local power</th><th>Network identity</th></tr>
<tr><td>LocalSystem (S-1-5-18)</td><td>Everything, more than Administrator</td><td>The computer account</td></tr>
<tr><td>NetworkService</td><td>Low</td><td>The computer account</td></tr>
<tr><td>LocalService</td><td>Low</td><td>Anonymous</td></tr>
<tr><td>Virtual account (NT SERVICE\\name)</td><td>Only what you grant</td><td>The computer account</td></tr>
<tr><td>gMSA (Module 4)</td><td>Only what you grant</td><td>Its own domain identity, auto-rotated password</td></tr></table>
<p>Inspect with <code>Get-Service</code>, <code>sc.exe qc &lt;name&gt;</code> and <code>services.msc</code>. A service you did not install, set to Automatic, running an unsigned binary from a user profile, is the shape of persistence.</p>
<h4>The registry</h4>
<p>The registry is a transactional hierarchical database. Five root keys: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE, HKEY_USERS, HKEY_CURRENT_USER (a link to <code>HKU\\&lt;your SID&gt;</code>), and the merged views HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT and HKEY_CURRENT_CONFIG. On disk each <strong>registry hive</strong> is a file: <code>SAM</code>, <code>SECURITY</code>, <code>SOFTWARE</code>, <code>SYSTEM</code> and <code>DEFAULT</code> in <code>C:\\Windows\\System32\\config</code>, plus <code>NTUSER.DAT</code> in each profile; <code>CurrentControlSet</code> points at one of the <code>ControlSet00x</code> keys. Values are typed (REG_SZ, REG_EXPAND_SZ, REG_MULTI_SZ, REG_DWORD, REG_BINARY). Tools: <code>regedit</code>, <code>reg.exe</code> and PowerShell's <code>HKLM:</code> drive. The keys both administrators and attackers care about are the auto-start points: <code>...\\CurrentVersion\\Run</code> and <code>RunOnce</code> under HKLM and HKCU, the Services key, Winlogon's <code>Userinit</code> and <code>Shell</code>, scheduled tasks and Image File Execution Options. Autoruns enumerates them all.</p>
<h4>NTFS and access control</h4>
<p><strong>NTFS</strong> stores every file as a record in the Master File Table, uses 4 KB clusters by default, journals metadata (<code>$LogFile</code>, <code>$UsnJrnl</code>) so crashes are survivable, and supports alternate data streams (the <code>Zone.Identifier</code> stream is the "mark of the web" behind SmartScreen; <code>dir /R</code> shows them), hard links, junctions, symbolic links, compression, EFS, quotas and Volume Shadow Copies. Every object carries a security descriptor: owner, DACL and SACL. The <strong>ACL</strong> rule: explicit entries beat inherited ones and, at the same level, Deny beats Allow; the SACL lists what to audit; inheritance flows from folders to children unless blocked. Read with <code>icacls</code> or <code>Get-Acl</code>; the Effective Access tab does the arithmetic. On a shared folder two ACLs apply, share and NTFS, and the <em>more restrictive</em> wins. Richer than Linux mode bits (Domain 3), slower to reason about — and misconfigured ACLs are a top finding in Windows assessments.</p>
<h4>Sysinternals: seeing the machine</h4>
<p>Mark Russinovich's free <strong>Sysinternals</strong> suite (learn.microsoft.com/sysinternals, runnable from <code>\\\\live.sysinternals.com\\tools</code>) is the x-ray. <em>Process Explorer</em> (v17.2): the process tree with parents, Verify Signatures and a VirusTotal column, a lower pane of handles and DLLs, purple rows for packed images. <em>Autoruns</em> (v14.3): every auto-start location, with "Hide Microsoft entries" to reveal the foreign ones. <em>Process Monitor</em> (v4.1): a live trace of file, registry, network and process events with filters and boot logging — the dynamic-analysis tool of Domain 11. Add <em>TCPView</em>, <em>Sigcheck</em>, <em>AccessChk</em>, <em>ProcDump</em> and <em>Sysmon</em> (Module 7). The triage recipe you will reuse: Process Explorer for "what runs and is it signed," Autoruns for "how does it come back," Procmon for "what does it touch."</p>
<h4>On the exams</h4>
<p>MD-102 tests device troubleshooting and diagnostics; AZ-802's "Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments" (15–20%) assumes you can read processes, services, logs and performance data. More importantly, this is the vocabulary every later module and every Windows security conversation presumes.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A laptop is slow after logon.</strong> Process Explorer, sorted by CPU, shows <code>svchost.exe</code> at 38% — but its path is <code>C:\\Users\\&lt;user&gt;\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost.exe</code>, its parent is <code>explorer.exe</code>, and Verify Signatures reports "(No signature was present in the subject)". Real svchost lives in <code>C:\\Windows\\System32</code> and is always a child of <code>services.exe</code>. Autoruns shows the same path under <code>HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run</code>, which explains why only this user's logons are slow and why HKLM looks clean. Procmon, filtered to that process, shows it writing a 2.1 MB file every 30 seconds to <code>%TEMP%</code> and querying DNS for a random-looking hostname — behavior, not a signature, convicts it. Response: isolate the machine, suspend (not kill) the process so memory can be captured, export the Run key as evidence, let Defender quarantine, reset the user's passwords, and file the hostname as an indicator for Module 7's detection pipeline.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Task Manager shows me everything that is running.\" It shows names, which anything can fake; Process Explorer adds the path, the parent, the signature and the handles, which are much harder to fake.","\"Administrator means kernel access.\" Administrators still run in user mode under a filtered token; reaching kernel mode requires a signed driver, which is exactly why driver signing and HVCI exist.","\"Share permissions and NTFS permissions add up.\" They intersect: the more restrictive of the two applies, and an explicit Deny beats any Allow at the same level."],
problems:[
{q:"A handle value is 0x2A8. Which handle-table entry is it, and why must handle values be multiples of 4?", a:"0x2A8 = 680; 680 ÷ 4 = entry 170. The low two bits are reserved for flags, so handle values step by 4."},
{q:"x64 Windows gives each process 128 TB of user address space. How many 4 KB pages is that?", a:"128 TB = 2^47 bytes; 2^47 ÷ 2^12 = 2^35 ≈ 34.4 billion pages (almost all unmapped, which is why page tables are sparse)."},
{q:"User Ana is in Finance (Allow Modify on \\\\FS01\\Budget, inherited) and in Interns (explicit Deny Write on the Q3 subfolder). Can she edit Q3\\plan.xlsx? Can she read it?", a:"Explicit Deny Write on Q3 beats the inherited Allow, so she cannot write; Read is not denied, so she can read."},
{q:"Diagnose: a process named lsass.exe is running from C:\\Users\\Public with parent cmd.exe. List three things that are wrong and what you do first.", a:"Real lsass.exe lives in System32, is started by wininit.exe, and is signed by Microsoft; a copy in Public under cmd.exe is masquerading. First isolate the host and capture memory/evidence, then remediate and reset credentials."},
{q:"A program starts at logon for one user but not for another on the same PC. Which two registry locations distinguish the cases, and which tool confirms it in one screen?", a:"HKCU\\...\\CurrentVersion\\Run (per user) versus HKLM\\...\\CurrentVersion\\Run (all users); Autoruns, with the Logon tab, shows both."}
],
checks:[{q:"Which file contains the kernel and the executive?",a:"ntoskrnl.exe."},{q:"Which process is the Service Control Manager, and which process holds logon credentials?",a:"services.exe; lsass.exe."},{q:"Where are the HKLM hive files stored on disk?",a:"C:\\Windows\\System32\\config (SAM, SECURITY, SOFTWARE, SYSTEM, DEFAULT)."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA how a double-click on an .exe becomes a process: the token it inherits, the handles it opens, the DLLs it loads through ntdll into the kernel, and how you would prove with Process Explorer that it is legitimate."},

{id:"p16m2", title:"PowerShell Mastery: Objects, the Pipeline, Scripting, Remoting and Automation", minutes:60,
outcomes:["State why PowerShell passes objects, not text, and use Get-Member to read any object.","Discover any command through Verb-Noun naming, Get-Command and Get-Help.","Write parameterized functions and modules with real error handling.","Remote into machines with WinRM and SSH, understand the ports and authentication, and schedule tasks safely.","Translate a Bash pipeline from Domain 3 into PowerShell and back."],
body:`<p>Why does every Windows administrator and every Windows attacker end up in <strong>PowerShell</strong>? Because it is the one interface that reaches everything — files, the registry, WMI/CIM, Active Directory, Intune through Microsoft Graph, Azure — with a single grammar. Bash (Domain 3) taught you to chain small programs that exchange text. PowerShell chains <strong>cmdlets</strong> that exchange .NET objects. That one design decision changes how you think.</p>
<h4>Objects, not text</h4>
<p>In Bash, <code>ps aux | grep nginx | awk '{print $2}'</code> parses columns and breaks if a column shifts. In PowerShell, <code>Get-Process nginx | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Id</code> asks the object for its <code>Id</code> property; nothing is parsed. <code>Get-Member</code> shows what an object carries — <code>Get-Process | Get-Member</code> lists properties (Id, CPU, WorkingSet64, Path) and methods (Kill). Formatting happens only at the screen, so <code>Format-Table</code> and <code>Format-List</code> go last: they emit formatting objects nothing else understands. The <strong>pipeline</strong> streams each object downstream as it is produced, so a million-line log never has to fit in memory.</p>
<h4>Verb-Noun and discoverability</h4>
<p>Every cmdlet is <code>Verb-Noun</code> with a verb from about a hundred approved ones (<code>Get-Verb</code>): Get, Set, New, Remove, Start, Stop, Enable, Disable, Invoke, Test, Export, Import. Knowing the noun is enough: <code>Get-Command -Noun Service</code> finds every service cmdlet. <code>Get-Help Get-Process -Full</code>, <code>-Examples</code> or <code>-Online</code> reads the manual (run <code>Update-Help</code> once). <code>-WhatIf</code> previews a destructive command; <code>-Confirm</code> asks first. Aliases (<code>ls</code>, <code>dir</code>, <code>cat</code>) are training wheels; scripts use full names.</p>
<h4>The pipeline toolkit</h4>
<pre>Get-ChildItem C:\\Logs -Recurse -File |
  Where-Object { $_.Length -gt 100MB -and $_.LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-30) } |
  Sort-Object Length -Descending |
  Select-Object FullName, @{n='GB';e={ [math]::Round($_.Length/1GB,2) }} |
  Export-Csv .\\old-big-logs.csv -NoTypeInformation</pre>
<p><code>Where-Object</code> filters, <code>Select-Object</code> projects (and computes properties), <code>Sort-Object</code>, <code>Group-Object</code> and <code>Measure-Object</code> (<code>-Sum</code>, <code>-Average</code>) shape, <code>ForEach-Object</code> acts per object, and <code>Export-Csv</code> or <code>ConvertTo-Json</code> hands results on. Operators are words: <code>-eq -ne -gt -lt -like -match -in</code>; <code>$_</code> is the current object; <code>KB MB GB TB</code> suffixes are built in. <em>Providers</em> expose stores as drives (<code>Get-PSDrive</code>: <code>HKLM:</code>, <code>Cert:</code>, <code>Env:</code>, <code>WSMan:</code>), so <code>Get-ChildItem HKLM:\\SOFTWARE</code> walks the registry with file verbs. Use <code>Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem</code>, not the old <code>Get-WmiObject</code>, which PowerShell 7 lacks.</p>
<h4>Two PowerShells</h4>
<p><em>Windows PowerShell 5.1</em> (<code>powershell.exe</code>, .NET Framework) ships in every Windows and is what Group Policy, many Microsoft modules and attackers' scripts target. <em>PowerShell 7.6</em> (<code>pwsh.exe</code>, .NET 10, the current Long Term Support release, generally available March 18, 2026) installs side by side with <code>winget install Microsoft.PowerShell</code>, runs on Linux and macOS, and loads Windows-only modules through a compatibility layer. Execution policy is Restricted on Windows clients and RemoteSigned on servers and in PowerShell 7; it prevents <em>accidental</em> script runs, not attackers — <code>-ExecutionPolicy Bypass</code> is one flag away. Real control is App Control for Business, Constrained Language Mode and logging.</p>
<h4>Scripting: functions, modules, errors</h4>
<pre>function Get-StaleLocalUser {
  [CmdletBinding()]
  param([int]$Days = 90)
  try {
    Get-LocalUser -ErrorAction Stop |
      Where-Object { $_.Enabled -and $_.LastLogon -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-$Days) }
  } catch { Write-Error "Local user query failed on $env:COMPUTERNAME : $_" }
}</pre>
<p>Typed <code>param()</code> blocks with defaults, <code>[CmdletBinding()]</code> for <code>-Verbose</code> and <code>-WhatIf</code>, comment-based help and <code>ValueFromPipeline</code> turn a function into a first-class cmdlet. Errors are <em>terminating</em> (stop the pipeline; <code>try/catch</code> sees them) or <em>non-terminating</em> (written to the error stream; execution continues); <code>-ErrorAction Stop</code> promotes the second into the first so <code>catch</code> fires. <code>$?</code> reports success; <code>$LASTEXITCODE</code> carries a native program's exit code. Package functions as a module (<code>.psm1</code> plus a <code>.psd1</code> manifest in <code>$env:PSModulePath</code>), install others from the PowerShell Gallery with <code>Install-PSResource</code> — after reading the code, because the Gallery is public — test with Pester, lint with PSScriptAnalyzer, edit in VS Code.</p>
<h4>Remoting and scheduled tasks</h4>
<p>Remoting rides <strong>WinRM</strong> (WS-Management over HTTP 5985 or HTTPS 5986), on by default on Windows Server and enabled with <code>Enable-PSRemoting</code> on clients. <code>Enter-PSSession SRV01</code> is one-to-one; <code>Invoke-Command -ComputerName SRV01,SRV02 -ScriptBlock { Get-Service W32Time }</code> fans out to 32 machines at a time and returns objects tagged with <code>PSComputerName</code>. In a domain the authentication is <strong>Kerberos</strong> (Module 4), mutual and ticket-based; between workgroup machines you need TrustedHosts or HTTPS with a certificate. The "second hop" (a remote session cannot forward your credential onward) is deliberate; solve it with resource-based constrained delegation or explicit credentials, never blanket CredSSP. <em>Just Enough Administration</em> publishes endpoints that expose only named cmdlets to a role — delegation without Domain Admin. PowerShell 7 also remotes over SSH (<code>Enter-PSSession -HostName linuxbox -UserName ops</code>). Scheduled tasks are the automation engine: <code>New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -Daily -At 06:00</code>, <code>New-ScheduledTaskAction -Execute pwsh.exe -Argument '-File C:\\Scripts\\audit.ps1'</code>, <code>Register-ScheduledTask</code>; run them as a gMSA or a dedicated low-privilege account, never your admin account — the task stores the identity, and creation is audited as event 4698.</p>
<h4>Logging: what defenders see</h4>
<p><strong>Group Policy</strong> or Intune can enable Module Logging, Script Block Logging (event 4104 in Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational records every script block after de-obfuscation) and Transcription; AMSI hands script content to Defender before it runs. The PowerShell that automates your audits is also the attacker's favorite tool, which is why Module 7 treats 4104 as a first-class signal.</p>
<h4>Bash and PowerShell, side by side</h4>
<table><tr><th>Task</th><th>Bash (Domain 3)</th><th>PowerShell</th></tr>
<tr><td>Big files</td><td><code>find . -size +100M</code></td><td><code>Get-ChildItem -Recurse | Where-Object Length -gt 100MB</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Field extraction</td><td><code>awk '{print $2}'</code></td><td>property access: <code>.Id</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Exit status</td><td><code>$?</code> (number)</td><td><code>$?</code> (boolean), <code>$LASTEXITCODE</code> (number)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Remote run</td><td><code>ssh host cmd</code></td><td><code>Invoke-Command -ComputerName host { cmd }</code></td></tr></table>
<h4>On the exams</h4>
<p>MD-102's "Optimize endpoint operations" domain (10–15%) names PowerShell and Microsoft Graph for automating Intune and extending compliance with scripts; AZ-802 expects remote management of servers with PowerShell and Windows Admin Center. Both show you cmdlets and ask what they do, so Verb-Noun fluency pays directly.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Audit local accounts on five machines in one command.</strong> From a domain-joined admin workstation: <code>Invoke-Command -ComputerName PC01,PC02,PC03,PC04,PC05 -ScriptBlock { Get-LocalUser | Select-Object Name, Enabled, PasswordLastSet, LastLogon; Get-LocalGroupMember Administrators | Select-Object Name, ObjectClass }</code>, then pipe to <code>Export-Csv</code>. Result: 5 hosts × 4 local accounts = 20 rows plus 13 Administrators members. Findings: PC03 has a local account "helpdesk" that is Enabled with PasswordLastSet in 2021 and a LastLogon of never — a stale backdoor-shaped account; PC01 and PC04 list a domain user directly in local Administrators instead of a group. Fix: disable the account (<code>Disable-LocalUser</code>), replace direct admin membership with a group managed through Intune or Group Policy, deploy Windows LAPS (Module 6) so the built-in Administrator has a unique rotating password, and schedule the audit weekly as a task running as a low-privilege service account. Total time: about twelve minutes, and the script is reusable on five hundred machines.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"PowerShell is cmd.exe with longer commands.\" It is a .NET object shell; the length comes from self-describing names you can discover with Get-Command and Get-Help.","\"Pipe to Format-Table, then keep processing.\" Format-* emits formatting objects; Export-Csv after it produces garbage. Format last.","\"Execution policy protects us from malicious scripts.\" It prevents accidents only; use App Control, Constrained Language Mode and Script Block Logging for actual control."],
problems:[
{q:"Write one pipeline that lists services set to start Automatically that are not running, showing Name and StartType.", a:"Get-Service | Where-Object { $_.StartType -eq 'Automatic' -and $_.Status -ne 'Running' } | Select-Object Name, StartType"},
{q:"Compute the total size in GB of all .iso files under D:\\ISOs, to two decimals, in one pipeline.", a:"[math]::Round((Get-ChildItem D:\\ISOs -Recurse -Filter *.iso | Measure-Object Length -Sum).Sum / 1GB, 2)"},
{q:"Invoke-Command to a workgroup PC fails with 'WinRM cannot process the request'. List the three most likely causes in order.", a:"WinRM not enabled (Enable-PSRemoting); port 5985/5986 blocked by the firewall profile (Public blocks it); no Kerberos in a workgroup, so the target must be in TrustedHosts or reached over HTTPS with credentials."},
{q:"Design error handling for a script that queries 50 servers so one unreachable server does not stop the run and failures are recorded.", a:"Loop or Invoke-Command per server inside try/catch with -ErrorAction Stop; in catch, append the server name and $_ to a log/CSV; use -ErrorVariable or $Error to collect; summarize successes/failures at the end."},
{q:"Translate: ps aux | grep -i chrome | wc -l", a:"(Get-Process -Name chrome -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).Count — or Get-Process | Where-Object Name -like '*chrome*' | Measure-Object | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Count"}
],
checks:[{q:"Default WinRM ports?",a:"5985 (HTTP) and 5986 (HTTPS)."},{q:"Which cmdlet reveals an object's properties and methods?",a:"Get-Member."},{q:"Current LTS PowerShell release and its runtime?",a:"PowerShell 7.6 on .NET 10 (GA March 2026); Windows PowerShell 5.1 remains built into Windows."}],
explain:"Teach ADA the object pipeline by converting a Bash one-liner into PowerShell, showing with Get-Member why no text parsing is needed, and then explaining what remoting adds and which port and authentication it uses."},

{id:"p16m3", title:"Windows Server: Editions, Roles, Core vs Desktop, DNS and DHCP, Files, Hyper-V and Licensing", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Compare Windows Server 2025 editions and the Server Core and Desktop Experience installation options.","Install and manage roles with Server Manager, PowerShell and Windows Admin Center.","Configure DNS and DHCP the Windows way and do scope arithmetic.","Deploy file and print services with the right permission model, and Hyper-V with the right generation and switches.","Do core-based licensing arithmetic and justify Standard versus Datacenter."],
body:`<p>Windows Server is the same NT kernel you met in Module 1, tuned for background work and sold with the roles that run a business: identity, names, addresses, files, printing, virtualization. The current release is <strong>Windows Server 2025</strong> (generally available November 1, 2024; mainstream support to November 2029, extended to November 2034). This module teaches you what to install, how to manage it without a mouse, and how to count the licenses — arithmetic that is tested and that real buyers get wrong.</p>
<h4>Editions and what 2025 added</h4>
<table><tr><th>Edition</th><th>Who</th><th>Key difference</th></tr>
<tr><td>Standard</td><td>Physical or lightly virtualized</td><td>2 virtual machines per full set of licenses; Storage Replica limited to one partnership and 2 TB</td></tr>
<tr><td>Datacenter</td><td>Virtualization hosts</td><td>Unlimited VMs, Storage Spaces Direct, software-defined networking, shielded VMs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Datacenter: Azure Edition</td><td>Azure and Azure Local only</td><td>Hotpatching at no cost, annual feature updates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Essentials</td><td>OEM-only small business</td><td>One small server, no CALs, 25 users / 50 devices</td></tr></table>
<p>Know 2025 feature by feature, because exams ask: Active Directory gets an optional 32k database page size, TLS 1.3 for LDAP, LDAP signing required by default for new deployments, a KDC that no longer issues RC4 ticket-granting tickets, and delegated Managed Service Accounts; SMB gets SMB over QUIC in Standard and Datacenter, signing required by default on outbound connections, NTLM blocking and an authentication rate limiter; Hyper-V gets GPU partitioning and limits of 4 PB of memory and 2,048 logical processors per host; Hotpatch arrives on-premises through Azure Arc; Windows LAPS can create the local account itself; DTrace, Bluetooth and Wi-Fi support appear.</p>
<h4>Server Core versus Desktop Experience</h4>
<p><strong>Server Core</strong> has no Explorer shell, no browser and no local MMC consoles: a command prompt, <code>sconfig</code> for first-boot settings, and remote management for everything else. The payoff is a smaller attack surface, fewer patches that need reboots, less memory and disk, and a server you administer like a Linux box — from PowerShell, RSAT consoles on your workstation, or Windows Admin Center. Choose Core for domain controllers, DNS/DHCP, file servers and Hyper-V hosts; choose <em>Desktop Experience</em> when a role needs the GUI on the box (Remote Desktop Session Host) or while learning. Decide at install time: since Windows Server 2016 you cannot switch afterwards. <strong>Windows Admin Center</strong> (free; version 2511, generally available May 2026, is the rebuilt "v2" gateway) is a browser console on a management PC (port 6516) or a gateway server (443) that manages servers, clusters, Hyper-V and Azure hybrid services and replaces most of Server Manager. RSAT ships as Features on Demand on Windows 11.</p>
<h4>Roles and features</h4>
<p>A <em>role</em> is a job (AD DS, DNS Server, DHCP Server, File and Storage Services, Hyper-V, Print and Document Services, Web Server, Remote Desktop Services, Active Directory Certificate Services, WSUS); a <em>feature</em> supports roles (Failover Clustering, BitLocker, Windows Server Backup). Install with Server Manager or <code>Install-WindowsFeature AD-Domain-Services, DNS -IncludeManagementTools</code>; list with <code>Get-WindowsFeature</code>. Promoting a <strong>domain controller</strong> is a second step (<code>Install-ADDSForest</code>, Module 4).</p>
<h4>DNS and DHCP, the Windows way</h4>
<p>Windows DNS is ordinary DNS (Domain 2) with one twist: <em>AD-integrated zones</em> are stored in the directory, replicated by AD replication, and accept <em>secure dynamic updates</em> so members register their own A and PTR records. Domain controllers register the SRV records clients use to find them (<code>_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.&lt;domain&gt;</code>). Point members only at domain DNS servers; send public names to <em>forwarders</em> or conditional forwarders; turn on <em>scavenging</em> (7-day no-refresh plus 7-day refresh) or stale records accumulate for years. <code>Resolve-DnsName</code> and <code>nslookup -type=SRV _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.corp.example.com</code> are the first tests when "the domain is broken" — it is DNS more often than not.</p>
<p>Windows DHCP speaks DORA (Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge) with a default lease of 8 days. You define a <em>scope</em>, <em>exclusions</em> for static devices, <em>reservations</em> by MAC and <em>options</em>: 003 router, 006 DNS servers, 015 domain name, 066/067 for PXE boot. A DHCP server must be <em>authorized</em> in Active Directory before it serves a domain network, routers between subnets need an IP helper pointing at it, and since Windows Server 2012 <em>DHCP failover</em> replicates a scope between two servers (hot standby or load balance). Scope arithmetic: a /24 has 254 usable addresses; exclude .1–.20 for infrastructure and reserve 10 printers, and 224 dynamic leases remain; 300 laptops visiting for a day each would exhaust it in two days at an 8-day lease, so shorten the lease to 1 day or widen the subnet.</p>
<h4>File and print services</h4>
<p>Shares are SMB 3.1.1 (encrypted, signed by default in 2025; SMB1 is gone). <code>New-SmbShare -Name Finance -Path D:\\Finance -FullAccess 'CORP\\FS-Finance-Admins' -ChangeAccess 'CORP\\FS-Finance-RW'</code>, then set <strong>NTFS</strong> permissions on the folder; the effective right is the more restrictive of share and NTFS, so the convention is Authenticated Users: Full on the share and precise NTFS below, with access-based enumeration so users see only what they may open. Scale with DFS Namespaces (<code>\\\\corp.example.com\\shares\\finance</code> across many servers) and DFS Replication; govern with File Server Resource Manager (quotas, file screens); recover with Shadow Copies for Shared Folders; pool disks with Storage Spaces; consider ReFS for large data volumes; extend to the cloud with Azure File Sync. Printing is the Print and Document Services role with the Print Management console and Point and Print restrictions, and the lesson of 2021's PrintNightmare (CVE-2021-34527): disable the Print Spooler on any server that does not print, above all on domain controllers. Universal Print moves the function to the cloud for Intune-managed devices.</p>
<h4>Hyper-V</h4>
<p><strong>Hyper-V</strong> is a type-1 hypervisor: enabling the role makes the host OS the "root partition" above the hypervisor, which is also what Windows 11 uses for Virtualization-based Security. Generation 2 VMs (UEFI, Secure Boot, virtual TPM) are the default, and Windows 11 guests need the vTPM. Disks are VHDX (up to 64 TB); Dynamic Memory lets a VM breathe between a minimum and a maximum. Production checkpoints are safe for servers; standard checkpoints save RAM state and belong in labs only — never on domain controllers you intend to roll back (Module 4 explains USN rollback). Switches are external (bridged), internal (host plus VMs) or private (VMs only); nested virtualization (<code>Set-VMProcessor -ExposeVirtualizationExtensions $true</code>) lets a VM host VMs, which is how you build a whole lab in one box. Lab sizing: a Core domain controller runs well on 2 vCPUs and 2–4 GB; a Windows 11 client wants 2 vCPUs and 4 GB; a 32 GB host runs two DCs, a file server and two clients.</p>
<h4>Licensing arithmetic</h4>
<p>Windows Server is licensed per physical core: every core on the host, minimum 8 per processor and 16 per server, sold as 16-core and 2-core packs. Microsoft's suggested price for a 16-core pack is $1,176 for Standard and $6,771 for Datacenter, so a Standard core is about $73.50 and a 2-core pack about $147. A fully licensed Standard host may run <em>two</em> VMs (plus the host OS if it only manages them); license the host again for each additional pair. Datacenter allows unlimited VMs. Every user or device touching a server also needs a Client Access License (CAL), and Remote Desktop needs RDS CALs on top. Newer paths: pay-as-you-go through Azure Arc at $33.58 per core per month, and the Hotpatch subscription at $1.50 per core per month for Arc-connected machines (free on Azure Edition). The evaluation ISO runs 180 days and converts to a licensed edition with <code>DISM /Online /Set-Edition</code> and a key. Break-even: a host with two 20-core processors needs 40 core licenses. Standard: 40 × $73.50 = $2,940 per two VMs. Datacenter: 40 × $423.19 = $16,928. Six VMs on Standard cost $8,820; twelve cost $17,640 — Datacenter wins at about twelve VMs on that host.</p>
<h4>On the exams</h4>
<p>AZ-802 spends four of its seven domains here: server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment (10–15%), virtual machines (10–15%), on-premises and hybrid networking including DNS and IP addressing (10–15%), and storage and file services including Azure Files (15–20%). Expect Windows Admin Center, Hyper-V generations and checkpoints, DHCP failover, DNS forwarders and share-versus-NTFS scenarios.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A two-server office, specified to the dollar.</strong> One host, two 12-core processors, 128 GB RAM. VMs: DC01 (Server Core: AD DS, DNS, DHCP) and FS01 (Core: File Server, DFS Namespace, Print Management with the spooler on, shadow copies at 07:00 and 12:00). Licenses: 24 cores → one 16-core pack ($1,176) plus four 2-core packs (4 × $147 = $588) = $1,764 of Standard, which covers exactly these two VMs; 25 user CALs on top. DHCP: scope 10.10.20.0/24, exclusion .1–.30, reservations for 6 printers, lease 8 days; options 003 = 10.10.20.1, 006 = 10.10.20.10 (DC01), 015 = corp.example.com. DNS: AD-integrated zone with secure updates, forwarders to the ISP or a filtering resolver, scavenging on. File shares: \\\\corp.example.com\\shares\\finance → FS01 with Authenticated Users: Full on the share and NTFS groups FS-Finance-RW (Modify) and FS-Finance-RO (Read). Management: Windows Admin Center on your workstation plus RSAT, both servers headless. Backups: Windows Server Backup of DC01's system state nightly to a share on separate storage. Lifetime: supported into 2034.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Datacenter is a faster or more featured operating system.\" Same binaries; it buys unlimited virtualization rights and software-defined datacenter features. Below about a dozen VMs per host, Standard is cheaper.","\"I can add the GUI to Server Core later.\" Not since Server 2016; the installation option is permanent, so manage Core remotely from day one.","\"Any machine can hand out DHCP leases on a domain network.\" A Windows DHCP server must be authorized in Active Directory; unauthorized Windows DHCP servers detect the condition and stop serving."],
problems:[
{q:"A host has two 24-core processors and will run 5 VMs. How many core licenses and packs for Standard, and what does it cost versus Datacenter?", a:"48 cores × 3 sets (5 VMs need 3 pairs) = 144 Standard core licenses = nine 16-core packs = $10,584; Datacenter 48 cores = three 16-core packs = $20,313. Standard wins."},
{q:"A /23 scope with 60 excluded addresses and an 8-day lease serves 600 devices that rotate daily. Will it hold? Fix it.", a:"A /23 has 510 usable; minus 60 = 450 dynamic addresses for 600 daily devices with 8-day leases — exhausted on day one. Shorten the lease to 8–24 hours and/or widen to a /22 (1,022 usable)."},
{q:"Assign Core or Desktop Experience and justify: (a) domain controller, (b) Hyper-V host, (c) RDS session host for 40 users, (d) a legacy app whose installer needs a GUI.", a:"(a) Core, (b) Core, (c) Desktop Experience (users need the shell), (d) Desktop Experience or install elsewhere; Core cannot be converted later."},
{q:"A Windows 11 guest refuses to install in a new VM. Which generation and settings does it need?", a:"Generation 2 with Secure Boot and a virtual TPM enabled (plus 4 GB RAM and 64 GB disk); Generation 1 lacks UEFI and vTPM."},
{q:"Design DNS for a branch office with 30 PCs, one domain controller, and a slow WAN: where do clients point, where do unknown names go, and what do you enable to keep the zone clean?", a:"Clients → the branch DC first, HQ DC second; DC forwards unresolved names to HQ or a filtering resolver via forwarders; AD-integrated zone with secure dynamic updates and scavenging (7/7 days) enabled."}
],
checks:[{q:"Minimum core licenses per server and per processor?",a:"16 per server, 8 per processor."},{q:"How many VMs does one full set of Standard licenses allow?",a:"Two (plus the host OS if it only manages VMs)."},{q:"Evaluation period of the Windows Server 2025 ISO?",a:"180 days."}],
explain:"Walk ADA through specifying a small office server: edition, Core versus Desktop, which roles on which VM, the DHCP scope math, the DNS forwarders, and the exact license count and price."},

{id:"p16m4", title:"Active Directory: Forests, Domains, Kerberos, Group Policy, FSMO Roles and Replication", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Explain forests, domains, trusts, sites, OUs and groups and the boundary each one actually is.","Trace Kerberos authentication step by step and contrast it with NTLM and its deprecation.","Compute Group Policy precedence and know the refresh timing and tools.","Name the five FSMO roles, what breaks when each is down, and how DNS locates domain controllers.","Describe replication (USNs, sites, intervals, tombstones) and design a small domain."],
body:`<p>Why does one directory service from 2000 still sit under most of the world's enterprises? Because <strong>Active Directory Domain Services</strong> does three jobs at once — a directory of identities (LDAP), authentication (<strong>Kerberos</strong> and NTLM) and configuration (Group Policy) — and ties them to DNS so a laptop can find a <strong>domain controller</strong> anywhere on earth. A pentester who cannot read AD (Domain 8) is blind in an enterprise; an administrator who cannot design it builds the weaknesses Module 7 describes.</p>
<h4>The database and its boundaries</h4>
<p>Every domain controller holds a copy of <code>ntds.dit</code> (an Extensible Storage Engine database in <code>C:\\Windows\\NTDS</code>) with partitions: <em>schema</em>, <em>configuration</em> (sites, topology), one <em>domain</em> partition per domain, and <em>application</em> partitions such as DNS zones. Objects are named by distinguished name (<code>CN=Ana Ruiz,OU=Users,DC=corp,DC=example,DC=com</code>), identified forever by a GUID and authorized by their security identifier. A <strong>forest</strong> is the security boundary: one schema, one configuration, one Enterprise Admins group. A <em>domain</em> is a replication, policy and naming boundary — not a security boundary, because a domain admin in a child domain can reach the forest. <em>Trusts</em> connect: parent–child trusts are automatic and transitive; forest trusts link forests, kept honest by SID filtering and selective authentication. <em>Sites</em> group well-connected subnets to shape replication and steer clients to a nearby DC. The design rule from Microsoft and from experience: one forest, one domain, named as a subdomain of a public domain you own (<code>corp.example.com</code>), never <code>.local</code>. The <em>global catalog</em> (ports 3268/3269) holds a partial copy of every domain for logon and search; make every DC a GC in a single domain.</p>
<h4>OUs, users, groups, service accounts</h4>
<p>An <strong>organizational unit</strong> exists for two reasons: to link Group Policy and to delegate administration (the help desk can reset passwords in OU=Users without touching OU=Servers). OUs do not grant permissions; <em>groups</em> do. Scopes: domain local (permissions on resources), global (people by role), universal (across domains). The pattern is AGDLP: Accounts into Global groups, Global groups into Domain Local groups, Domain Local groups get the Permissions. High-value built-ins: Domain Admins (RID 512), Enterprise Admins (519), Schema Admins (518), Administrators (544), and the protective <em>Protected Users</em> group (Module 7). Services should run as <em>group Managed Service Accounts</em> (gMSA): AD generates a random 240-byte password and rotates it every 30 days; Windows Server 2025 adds delegated Managed Service Accounts to migrate legacy accounts. The <code>krbtgt</code> account's key signs every ticket in the domain — rotate it (twice, with replication between) at least yearly and after any suspected compromise. Useful attributes: <code>userAccountControl</code>, <code>pwdLastSet</code>, and <code>lastLogonTimestamp</code> (replicated only when 9–14 days stale, which is what makes stale-account reports possible).</p>
<h4>Kerberos, step by step</h4>
<p>Every DC runs the Key Distribution Center on TCP/UDP 88. (1) <em>AS-REQ</em>: the client sends the username and a timestamp encrypted with a key derived from the user's password. (2) <em>AS-REP</em>: the KDC returns a <em>ticket-granting ticket</em> (TGT), encrypted with the krbtgt key so only DCs can read it, plus a session key. (3) <em>TGS-REQ</em>: to reach a service the client presents the TGT and the service's <em>Service Principal Name</em> (<code>cifs/fs01.corp.example.com</code>). (4) <em>TGS-REP</em>: the KDC returns a <em>service ticket</em> encrypted with the service account's key, carrying a Privilege Attribute Certificate listing the user's group SIDs. (5) <em>AP-REQ</em>: the client hands the ticket to the service, which decrypts it with its own key — no password crosses the wire and the DC is not contacted again. Defaults: TGT lifetime 10 hours, renewable 7 days; service tickets 600 minutes; clock skew tolerance 5 minutes, which makes time synchronization (the PDC emulator is the domain's time source; <code>w32tm /resync</code>) a Kerberos dependency. Encryption types: AES256 (etype 18) and AES128 (17) are right; RC4 (23) is legacy, and a Windows Server 2025 KDC no longer issues RC4 TGTs. Kerberos needs DNS SRV records and correct SPNs (<code>setspn -L</code>); when either is wrong, Windows silently falls back to NTLM.</p>
<p><strong>NTLM</strong> is that fallback: a challenge–response protocol (NTLMv2 today) in which the server forwards the response to a DC for validation (event 4776). It has no mutual authentication and can be relayed, which is why SMB signing and LDAP channel binding exist. Microsoft deprecated NTLM in June 2024 and in February 2026 published a three-phase plan: enhanced auditing now (Windows 11 24H2 and Server 2025), new tools in the second half of 2026 (IAKerb and a local KDC so Kerberos works by IP address and for local accounts), and NTLM disabled by default in the next Windows Server release and its client. Your job today is phase one: audit where NTLM is still used and remove the reasons.</p>
<h4>Group Policy</h4>
<p>A <strong>Group Policy</strong> Object is two halves: a container in AD and a template folder in <strong>SYSVOL</strong> (<code>\\\\corp.example.com\\SYSVOL</code>, replicated by DFS-R). Link GPOs to sites, domains and OUs; clients process Local → Site → Domain → OU → child OU, and the last writer wins, so the OU closest to the object has the final word. Two modifiers change the arithmetic: <em>Block Inheritance</em> on an OU stops higher GPOs; <em>Enforced</em> on a link overrides a block and wins all ties. Security filtering and WMI filters target subsets; <em>loopback processing</em> applies user settings by computer location. Refresh runs every 90 minutes plus a random offset of up to 30 minutes on clients and every 5 minutes on domain controllers; some settings wait for a logon or reboot. <code>gpupdate /force</code> and <code>gpresult /h report.html</code> show what applied and why. Keep the Default Domain Policy for password and Kerberos settings only, create purpose-named GPOs for everything else, and keep ADMX templates in the Central Store (<code>SYSVOL\\domain\\Policies\\PolicyDefinitions</code>). Intune's Group Policy analytics (Module 5) reads a GPO backup and reports which settings have a cloud equivalent.</p>
<h4>DNS dependency and FSMO roles</h4>
<p>Clients find DCs through the <em>DC Locator</em>: a DNS query for <code>_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.corp.example.com</code>, then an LDAP ping to pick a DC in their site. No DNS, no domain. Multi-master replication handles most writes, but five operations need a single owner — the <strong>FSMO roles</strong>: <em>Schema Master</em> and <em>Domain Naming Master</em> (one per forest), <em>RID Master</em> (hands each DC pools of 500 relative IDs so SIDs never collide), <em>PDC Emulator</em> (authoritative time, password-change forwarding, lockouts, default GPO editing target — the busiest role) and <em>Infrastructure Master</em> (cross-domain references; irrelevant when every DC is a GC). <code>netdom query fsmo</code> lists them; transfer roles gracefully, <em>seize</em> only when the holder is gone for good, and never bring the old holder back.</p>
<h4>Replication</h4>
<p>Each DC stamps changes with an Update Sequence Number and tracks partners with a high-watermark and an up-to-dateness vector, so changes replicate once. Inside a site a DC notifies partners 15 seconds after a change (3 seconds to each subsequent partner); between sites, <em>site links</em> carry a cost, a schedule and an interval (default 180 minutes, minimum 15) with compressed traffic through bridgehead servers. The Knowledge Consistency Checker rebuilds the topology every 15 minutes; urgent changes (lockouts, password changes) jump the queue. Deleted objects become <em>tombstones</em> kept 180 days; enable the AD Recycle Bin (forest functional level 2008 R2 or higher) so a deleted OU comes back intact. <code>repadmin /replsummary</code>, <code>repadmin /showrepl</code> and <code>dcdiag</code> are the health checks. The cardinal sin is <em>USN rollback</em>: a DC restored from a VM snapshot reuses USNs its partners already saw, so they silently ignore its changes; Hyper-V's VM-Generation ID guards against it, but the rule stands — restore DCs from system-state backups, never from snapshots.</p>
<h4>Design a small domain</h4>
<p>Forty users, two sites. Forest and domain <code>corp.example.com</code>, functional level Windows Server 2025. Two Server Core DCs at HQ (both DNS and GC, FSMO roles split), one DC at the branch (read-only if the site is physically insecure); sites HQ and Branch with their subnets and a 15-minute site link. OUs: <code>Tier0</code> (admin accounts, PAWs), <code>Servers</code>, <code>Workstations</code>, <code>Users</code>, <code>Groups</code>, <code>ServiceAccounts</code>, <code>Staging</code>. Groups by AGDLP (<code>GG-Finance</code> → <code>DL-FS-Finance-RW</code>). GPOs: Baseline-Workstations, Baseline-Servers, Tier0-Restrictions (deny logon of admin accounts on lower tiers), and a 14-plus-character password policy. All services on gMSAs, admins in Protected Users, time from the PDC emulator to a reliable NTP source, nightly system-state backups, and monitoring of 4740 lockouts and replication health. That is a domain you could defend.</p>
<h4>On the exams</h4>
<p>"Deploy and manage AD DS" is the largest AZ-802 domain at 20–25%: deploying DCs, multi-site and multi-forest environments, security principals and domain-based Group Policy. MD-102 touches AD through hybrid join and Group Policy analytics. Know the FSMO roles, the Kerberos defaults, the GPO order and the replication intervals cold.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Trace one double-click.</strong> At 08:02 Ana opens <code>\\\\fs01\\finance</code>. Her laptop already holds a TGT from logon (AS exchange at 07:58, valid until 17:58). It asks DNS for the SRV record of a DC in site HQ, sends a TGS-REQ for <code>cifs/fs01.corp.example.com</code>, and receives a service ticket whose PAC lists her SIDs, including <code>GG-Finance</code>. The SMB session carries the ticket (AP-REQ); FS01 decrypts it with its computer account key, builds her access token, and evaluates the share ACL (Authenticated Users: Full) and then the NTFS ACL (DL-FS-Finance-RW: Modify). Access granted, with no DC contact. On the DC the Security log shows 4769 (service ticket requested, etype 0x12 = AES256). Variant: a laptop whose clock drifted 7 minutes gets KRB_AP_ERR_SKEW, the share fails, and Windows may fall back to NTLM (4776 on the DC). Fix: <code>w32tm /resync</code>, and ask why the machine stopped syncing to the PDC emulator.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"The domain is the security boundary.\" The forest is; any child-domain admin can escalate to the forest, which is why one domain per forest is the modern default.","\"Put users in OUs to give them access.\" OUs link policy and delegate administration; access comes from groups (AGDLP).","\"Group Policy applies as soon as I click OK.\" Clients refresh every 90 minutes plus up to 30 random minutes; some settings wait for a logon or reboot; gpupdate /force shortcuts the wait."],
problems:[
{q:"GPO A (domain, Enforced) sets the screen lock to 15 minutes; GPO B (OU=Sales, Block Inheritance) sets 60; GPO C (OU=Sales\\Field) sets 30. What does a Field laptop get, and why?", a:"15 minutes: Enforced links override Block Inheritance and win conflicts over lower OUs; without Enforced the answer would be 30 (closest OU wins)."},
{q:"A change made in Site A must reach Site C via Site B over default site links. Worst-case latency?", a:"Up to 180 minutes per hop × 2 hops = 6 hours (plus intra-site seconds); shorten the site-link interval to 15 minutes to cut it to about 30 minutes."},
{q:"The RID Master has been offline for a week. Which operation fails first and roughly when?", a:"Creating new security principals fails when a DC exhausts its current pool of 500 RIDs (and the standby pool); busy DCs run out first. Seize the role only if the old holder is never returning."},
{q:"Kerberos works for most users but one laptop authenticates to a file server with NTLM only. Give three causes in diagnostic order.", a:"Clock skew beyond 5 minutes; the client is addressing the server by IP or a CNAME without an SPN; DNS/SPN misregistration (setspn -L, missing SRV records). Check 4776 vs 4769 on the DC."},
{q:"Design the OU and group structure for a 60-person company with Finance, Sales and IT, two file shares per department, and a help desk that may reset non-admin passwords.", a:"OUs: Tier0, Servers, Workstations, Users (with Finance/Sales/IT sub-OUs), Groups, ServiceAccounts, Staging. Global groups per department; Domain Local groups per share per permission (DL-FS-Finance-RW/RO); delegate Reset Password on OU=Users (not Tier0) to GG-HelpDesk."}
],
checks:[{q:"Name the five FSMO roles.",a:"Schema Master, Domain Naming Master (forest); RID Master, PDC Emulator, Infrastructure Master (domain)."},{q:"Default Group Policy refresh on clients and DCs?",a:"90 minutes plus a 0–30 minute random offset; 5 minutes on DCs."},{q:"Which port does Kerberos use and what is the default TGT lifetime?",a:"TCP/UDP 88; 10 hours (renewable 7 days)."}],
explain:"Explain Kerberos to ADA as a story with a TGT and a service ticket, say exactly where the krbtgt and service keys are used, and then explain why clock skew and DNS SRV records can break it while NTLM still works."},

{id:"p16m5", title:"Identity and Endpoint Management: Entra ID, Hybrid Identity, Intune, Autopilot and Conditional Access", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Contrast Microsoft Entra ID with Active Directory and know the licensing tiers.","Explain hybrid identity: Connect Sync, Cloud Sync, PHS, PTA, federation and the three device join states.","Describe Intune enrollment, compliance, configuration, apps and remote actions.","Plan a Windows Autopilot deployment and choose the mode.","Design Conditional Access with MFA and Windows Hello for Business, and map it all to MD-102."],
body:`<p>How do you manage 300 laptops that never touch the office network? Not with Group Policy and a domain controller behind a firewall. Microsoft's answer since 2015 is cloud identity (<strong>Microsoft Entra ID</strong>, called Azure Active Directory until 2023) plus cloud device management (<strong>Intune</strong>), stitched together by policies that judge every sign-in. This is the MD-102 exam in one module, and it is where the Kerberos world of Module 4 meets OAuth tokens.</p>
<h4>Entra ID is not Active Directory in the cloud</h4>
<p>Active Directory is hierarchical (OUs), speaks LDAP and Kerberos, and manages machines with Group Policy. Entra ID is a flat multi-tenant directory that speaks HTTPS: OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect issue access, ID and refresh tokens; SAML covers older apps. There are no OUs and no GPOs; <em>groups</em> (including dynamic membership rules such as "all devices where deviceOSType equals Windows") drive assignment, and Intune replaces Group Policy. Tiers: <em>Free</em> (users, groups, Security Defaults), <em>P1</em> (list price $6 per user per month: Conditional Access, dynamic groups, self-service password reset with writeback), <em>P2</em> ($9: Identity Protection risk signals, Privileged Identity Management for just-in-time admin). Microsoft 365 Business Premium, E3 and E5 bundle P1 or P2 with Intune Plan 1 ($8 alone).</p>
<h4>Hybrid identity</h4>
<p>Most organizations keep AD for servers and legacy apps and project it into Entra ID. <em>Microsoft Entra Connect Sync</em> runs on a domain-joined server (V2 is mandatory; V1 was retired August 31, 2022) and synchronizes users, groups and devices every 30 minutes. <em>Entra Cloud Sync</em> is the lightweight, cloud-managed agent Microsoft calls the future of synchronization; prefer it for new deployments unless you need a feature it lacks (device writeback, some filtering). Sign-in methods: <em>password hash synchronization</em> (PHS; a salted, iterated hash of the on-premises hash is uploaded — Microsoft's recommended default, and leaked-credential detection works), <em>pass-through authentication</em> (PTA; lightweight agents validate against AD in real time, nothing stored in the cloud) or <em>federation</em> through AD FS (the legacy path most organizations are leaving). Devices relate to Entra ID in three ways: <em>Entra registered</em> (personal BYOD with a work account), <em>Entra joined</em> (corporate, cloud-only — the target for new hardware) and <em>Hybrid Entra joined</em> (domain-joined and registered in the cloud through Connect Sync, so Group Policy and Conditional Access both apply — the bridge for existing fleets). <em>Entra Kerberos</em> issues Kerberos tickets from the cloud so Windows Hello users reach on-premises file shares; <em>Entra Domain Services</em> provides a managed domain for apps that still need LDAP.</p>
<h4>Intune: management at scale</h4>
<p>Intune is the MDM/MAM service. Windows enrolls automatically when it joins Entra ID (the tenant's MDM enrollment setting), or through Group Policy and co-management with Configuration Manager for domain-joined fleets; Apple devices through Apple Business Manager; Android through Android Enterprise. Four kinds of policy flow down: <em>compliance policies</em> (BitLocker on, Defender active, TPM present, minimum OS version — producing the compliant signal Conditional Access consumes, with a grace period you define); <em>configuration profiles</em> (the Settings Catalog, ADMX import, Group Policy analytics); <em>endpoint security</em> (antivirus, disk encryption, firewall, attack surface reduction, EDR onboarding, security baselines); and <em>apps</em> (Win32 packaged as .intunewin, line-of-business MSI, Store, Microsoft 365 Apps, plus app protection policies for unmanaged phones). Updates ship as update rings, feature update policies, Autopatch and Hotpatch (Module 6). Remote actions: sync, restart, retire, wipe, rotate BitLocker and LAPS keys, collect diagnostics, run device queries in KQL. The Intune Suite add-on brings Endpoint Privilege Management (temporary elevation instead of local admin), Remote Help, Cloud PKI, Advanced Analytics, Microsoft Tunnel and the Enterprise App Catalog. Automate with the <code>Microsoft.Graph</code> module (<code>Connect-MgGraph</code>, then <code>Get-MgDeviceManagementManagedDevice</code>) — Module 2 applied to the cloud. A free 30-day Intune trial tenant exists and is Lab 5.</p>
<h4>Windows Autopilot</h4>
<p><strong>Windows Autopilot</strong> turns a factory-sealed laptop into a managed device without imaging. The reseller or you upload the hardware hash (<code>Get-WindowsAutopilotInfo -Online</code>); you assign a profile; the user unboxes, connects, signs in, and the Out-of-Box Experience joins Entra ID, enrolls in Intune, applies policies and installs required apps while an Enrollment Status Page shows progress. Modes: <em>user-driven</em> (the user signs in; the common case), <em>pre-provisioning</em> (IT or the reseller presses the Windows key five times to apply device-targeted apps first, so the user's phase takes minutes) and <em>self-deploying</em> (no user — kiosks and shared devices; needs TPM 2.0 attestation). Since 2024, <em>Autopilot device preparation</em> policies (the "v2" flow) coexist with classic deployment profiles, and MD-102 asks you to choose between them. Device-name templates (<code>LAP-%SERIAL%</code>) and "Autopilot for existing devices" round it out.</p>
<h4>Conditional Access, MFA and Windows Hello</h4>
<p><strong>Conditional Access</strong> is the policy engine: <em>if</em> (user or group, cloud app, device platform, named location, device state, sign-in or user risk) <em>then</em> (block, or grant requiring MFA, a compliant device, a hybrid-joined device, an approved app or a password change). Build in report-only mode, exclude two emergency "break-glass" accounts from every policy, and start with three policies: require MFA for all users, require a compliant device for Microsoft 365, block legacy authentication. Tenants without P1 get <em>Security Defaults</em> free, and Microsoft now enforces MFA for the Azure portal and admin centers regardless. MFA methods rank by phishing resistance: passkeys (FIDO2) and Windows Hello for Business resist phishing; Authenticator with number matching resists push fatigue; SMS and voice are last resorts. <em>Windows Hello for Business</em> replaces the password with a key pair generated in the device's TPM: the PIN or biometric unlocks the private key locally, the public key is registered with Entra ID (or AD), and a signed challenge — not a secret — crosses the wire. Trust models: <em>cloud Kerberos trust</em> (recommended; Entra Kerberos issues tickets, no certificates), <em>key trust</em>, <em>certificate trust</em>. Supporting controls: Entra Password Protection bans common and custom passwords (an agent extends it to domain controllers), Smart Lockout (10 failed attempts by default) resists spraying, and Identity Protection (P2) raises risk when credentials appear in a leak.</p>
<h4>The MD-102 skill set</h4>
<p>Exam MD-102 awards Microsoft 365 Certified: Endpoint Administrator Associate. The version effective October 27, 2026 measures: Prepare infrastructure for devices (20–25%: join types, enrollment, roles, compliance, Conditional Access, Windows Hello, Windows LAPS), Manage and maintain devices (25–30%: Autopilot, Windows 365 Cloud PCs, configuration profiles, Intune Suite, remote actions), Protect devices (15–20%: antivirus, BitLocker, firewall, attack surface reduction, Defender for Endpoint, App Control, update rings, Autopatch, Hotpatch), Manage and secure applications (15–20%) and Optimize endpoint operations with automation, monitoring and reporting (10–15%: PowerShell and Graph, Security Copilot agents, Endpoint analytics, remediation scripts). It is 100 minutes, $165 in the US, proctored at a Pearson VUE center or online, scored to 700 of 1,000, and renewed yearly with a free online assessment. Take it first: it certifies the half of Windows administration every employer now expects.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Twenty-five new laptops, zero imaging.</strong> The reseller registers 25 hardware hashes with a group tag <code>Sales</code>; a dynamic device group picks them up. Autopilot user-driven profile: Entra join, skip privacy and EULA pages, name template <code>SAL-%SERIAL%</code>, standard user (no local admin). Enrollment Status Page blocks until the required apps install. Policies assigned to the group: compliance (BitLocker, TPM 2.0, Defender real-time protection, Windows 11 24H2 or later, 3-day grace), endpoint security (BitLocker XTS-AES 256 with key escrow, firewall on all profiles, ASR rules in block mode, Defender for Endpoint onboarding), Windows LAPS, update ring (quality deferral 7 days, deadline 5 days), Microsoft 365 Apps. Conditional Access: all users → all cloud apps → require MFA and a compliant device, break-glass accounts excluded, legacy authentication blocked. Each user unboxes, connects, signs in once, registers Windows Hello (PIN plus fingerprint) and Authenticator; about 25 minutes per laptop, all 25 in parallel. The next morning the Intune compliance report shows 25/25 compliant and the Conditional Access sign-in logs show 25 "Success — compliant device" entries.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Entra ID is just Active Directory hosted in Azure.\" It is a flat, HTTPS/OAuth directory with no OUs, Kerberos or Group Policy; Intune and Conditional Access replace those functions.","\"Hybrid join is the modern end state.\" It is the bridge for existing domain-joined fleets; Microsoft's target for new devices is Entra join plus Intune.","\"MFA is MFA.\" SMS codes are phishable; passkeys and Windows Hello for Business are phishing-resistant and should be the default for administrators."],
problems:[
{q:"Design the first three Conditional Access policies for a 50-person firm with Entra ID P1, and state what you exclude from each.", a:"(1) All users → all apps → require MFA; (2) all users → Microsoft 365 → require compliant device; (3) block legacy authentication. Exclude two break-glass accounts (strong passwords, monitored) from all three; roll out in report-only first."},
{q:"Choose the join type: (a) new sales laptops, (b) a 2018 fleet still needing Group Policy, (c) employees' personal phones, (d) kiosks in a lobby.", a:"(a) Entra joined with Autopilot user-driven; (b) Hybrid Entra joined via Connect Sync; (c) Entra registered with app protection policies; (d) Entra joined via Autopilot self-deploying (TPM 2.0 attestation) with a kiosk profile."},
{q:"A device shows Not compliant and the user is blocked from Outlook. Give the diagnostic path.", a:"Intune → device → compliance policy details to see the failing setting (often BitLocker not yet reported or OS version); check the grace period; run Sync; verify the Conditional Access sign-in log shows 'requires compliant device'; fix the setting (for example start encryption) and re-evaluate."},
{q:"Compute the monthly identity cost for 40 users who need Conditional Access and Intune, comparing P1 + Intune Plan 1 with Business Premium at $22.", a:"P1 ($6) + Intune Plan 1 ($8) = $14 × 40 = $560/month for identity and management only; Business Premium $22 × 40 = $880/month includes Office apps, Exchange, Defender for Business and Intune — usually the better deal if they need Office anyway."},
{q:"Explain why Windows Hello for Business is phishing-resistant and a strong password with SMS MFA is not.", a:"Hello signs a challenge with a TPM-bound private key tied to the legitimate origin; nothing reusable crosses the wire and a fake site cannot complete it. A password and SMS code are both secrets a user can be tricked into typing into a proxy site."}
],
checks:[{q:"Minimum Entra license for Conditional Access?",a:"Entra ID P1 (Security Defaults are the free alternative)."},{q:"Three Autopilot deployment modes?",a:"User-driven, pre-provisioning, self-deploying."},{q:"MD-102 duration, price and certification name?",a:"100 minutes, $165 (US), Microsoft 365 Certified: Endpoint Administrator Associate."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA how a brand-new laptop becomes a compliant, policy-managed device through Autopilot, Entra join and Intune, and how Conditional Access then uses that compliance and MFA to decide whether Outlook opens."},

{id:"p16m6", title:"Hardening, Patching and Troubleshooting: Auditing, Defender, BitLocker, LAPS, Firewall, Updates, Backup and Performance", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Configure advanced audit policy, size the Security log and read the event IDs that matter.","Deploy Microsoft Defender, Credential Guard, LSA protection and security baselines.","Encrypt with BitLocker and manage local admin passwords with Windows LAPS.","Choose between WSUS, Windows Update for Business, Autopatch and Hotpatch and design update rings.","Back up and restore Windows and domain controllers correctly, and troubleshoot performance with counters."],
body:`<p>A built domain is a target; a hardened, patched, monitored and backed-up domain is a system you can defend. This module is the operational core of both exams and of every blue-team job: what to log, what to turn on, how to keep machines current, how to recover, and how to find out why a machine is slow. Nothing here is exotic — the difficulty is doing all of it, everywhere, every month.</p>
<h4>Event Viewer and audit policy</h4>
<p>The <strong>Event Viewer</strong> shows the classic logs — Application, Security, System, Setup — and hundreds of Applications and Services Logs such as Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational and Sysmon/Operational. The Security log fills only when <em>audit policy</em> says so: use Advanced Audit Policy Configuration (Group Policy or the Intune Settings Catalog), verify with <code>auditpol /get /category:*</code>, and enable at least logon/logoff, account logon, account management, process creation <em>with command line</em>, policy change and, on domain controllers, directory service access. IDs to read without looking up:</p>
<table><tr><th>ID</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Why you care</th></tr>
<tr><td>4624 / 4625</td><td>Logon succeeded / failed (type 2 interactive, 3 network, 10 RDP)</td><td>Who, from where, how; failures in bulk = spraying</td></tr>
<tr><td>4648 / 4672</td><td>Explicit-credential logon / special privileges assigned</td><td>Lateral movement; an admin where none should be</td></tr>
<tr><td>4688</td><td>Process created (with command line)</td><td>The backbone of detection</td></tr>
<tr><td>4720 / 4728 / 4732</td><td>User created / added to global or local group</td><td>Privilege changes</td></tr>
<tr><td>4740</td><td>Account locked out</td><td>Bursts = attack or broken service</td></tr>
<tr><td>4768 / 4769 / 4771 / 4776</td><td>TGT / service ticket / Kerberos pre-auth failure / NTLM validation</td><td>Kerberos health and abuse; where NTLM still lives</td></tr>
<tr><td>7045 / 4698 / 1102</td><td>New service / new scheduled task / audit log cleared</td><td>Persistence and track-covering</td></tr>
<tr><td>4104</td><td>PowerShell script block</td><td>What the script actually did</td></tr></table>
<p>Sizing is arithmetic: the Security log defaults to 20 MB. A domain controller writing 50 events per second at about 600 bytes each produces 30 KB/s, roughly 2.6 GB a day, so a 20 MB log wraps in about eleven minutes. Set 1 GB or more on DCs (Microsoft's baseline floor is 192 MB) and forward events off the box with Windows Event Forwarding or an agent to a SIEM (Wazuh is free; Microsoft Sentinel is the Azure option), because an attacker with admin rights can clear a local log but not one already shipped. Query with <code>Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4625; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)}</code>.</p>
<h4>Microsoft Defender and the platform protections</h4>
<p>Defender Antivirus is built in: cloud-delivered protection, Tamper Protection, controlled folder access against ransomware, network protection, and <em>attack surface reduction</em> rules such as "Block Office applications from creating child processes" and "Block credential stealing from LSASS" — audit first, read the events, then block. <code>Get-MpComputerStatus</code> and <code>Set-MpPreference</code> manage it locally; Intune or Group Policy at scale. Defender for Endpoint (Plan 1 and 2) adds EDR — device timelines, isolation, live response, KQL hunting — onboarded by an Intune policy. Platform protections to expect on modern hardware: Virtualization-based Security with hypervisor-protected code integrity, <em>Credential Guard</em> (on by default on Windows 11 22H2+ Enterprise and Education; isolates LSASS secrets in a virtual container), <em>LSA protection</em> (on by default for new Windows 11 22H2+ installs; stops unsigned code reading LSASS memory), Secure Boot, and Smart App Control or App Control for Business for allow-listing. Apply the Microsoft Security Baselines (Security Compliance Toolkit with LGPO, or the Intune baseline profiles) and document every deviation.</p>
<h4>BitLocker</h4>
<p><strong>BitLocker</strong> encrypts volumes with XTS-AES 128 by default (256 by policy) and seals the key to the TPM 2.0 so the disk opens only on its own machine with an untampered boot chain; add a pre-boot PIN for high-value laptops. The 48-digit recovery password must be escrowed to Entra ID or Active Directory before encryption completes (an Intune disk encryption policy or the GPO "Store BitLocker recovery information in AD DS") or a forgotten PIN is a wiped laptop. Windows 11 24H2 turns on Device Encryption by default on clean installs, Home edition included. Operate with <code>manage-bde -status</code>, <code>Get-BitLockerVolume</code> and <code>Suspend-BitLocker -RebootCount 1</code> before firmware updates; rotate recovery keys from Intune after any disclosure. It protects data at rest on a lost device; a running, unlocked machine is protected by its logon, not by BitLocker.</p>
<h4>Windows LAPS</h4>
<p>The same local Administrator password on every PC is the oldest lateral-movement highway in Windows. <strong>Windows LAPS</strong> (built into Windows 11 22H2+, Windows 10 since April 2023 and Windows Server 2019+) gives each machine a unique random password, rotates it (default every 30 days, optionally after each use) and backs it up to Entra ID or Active Directory (extend the schema once with <code>Update-LapsADSchema</code>). Windows Server 2025 adds managed-account creation with randomized names, passphrases (<code>EatYummyCaramelCandy</code>-style, with a readability option) and post-authentication actions that reset the password and sign the account out. Configure through Intune (Endpoint security → Account protection) or Group Policy; retrieve with <code>Get-LapsADPassword</code> or the Entra device page. The legacy Microsoft LAPS add-on is deprecated.</p>
<h4>Windows Firewall</h4>
<p>The host firewall has three profiles — Domain, Private, Public — and blocks inbound while allowing outbound. Manage with <code>wf.msc</code>, <code>netsh advfirewall</code>, <code>New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'WinRM from Mgmt' -Direction Inbound -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 5985 -RemoteAddress 10.10.99.0/24 -Action Allow</code>, or centrally through Group Policy and Intune. Domain 2 principles apply: scope every inbound rule to source subnets (WinRM and RDP only from the management network), log dropped packets while tuning, and treat the host firewall as the segmentation that stops one compromised workstation from reaching its neighbors over 445.</p>
<h4>Patching</h4>
<p>Security updates arrive on Patch Tuesday as cumulative updates; feature updates arrive yearly (Windows 11 version 26H2 began rolling out September 29, 2026 as a small enablement package). Windows 10 left support on October 14, 2025; consumer Extended Security Updates ended October 13, 2026, and commercial ESU costs $61, $122 and $244 per device for years one to three. Four delivery models: <em>WSUS</em> (an on-premises approval server, deprecated since September 20, 2024 — still in Windows Server 2025 and still receiving fixes but gaining no features, with the removal of driver synchronization postponed indefinitely in April 2025; fine for a lab, not a career); <em>Windows Update for Business</em> (devices pull from Microsoft under Intune update rings — quality deferral up to 30 days, feature deferral up to 365, deadlines and grace periods, Delivery Optimization so peers share downloads); <em>Windows Autopatch</em> (Microsoft runs the rings for E3/E5 tenants); and <em>Hotpatch</em> (security updates applied in memory without a reboot on Windows 11 Enterprise 24H2 and on Windows Server 2025 via Azure Arc at $1.50 per core per month, with reboots only in the baseline months of January, April, July and October — four a year instead of twelve). Arc-connected servers use Azure Update Manager. Ring design is risk management: 1% pilot on day 0, 10% on day 3, everyone by day 7 with a deadline; third-party applications through winget or the Intune Enterprise App Catalog.</p>
<h4>Backup and recovery</h4>
<p>Follow 3-2-1: three copies, two media, one offline or immutable so ransomware cannot encrypt the backup too. Windows Server Backup (<code>wbadmin start systemstatebackup -backupTarget:E:</code>) captures a domain controller's <em>system state</em> — the AD database, SYSVOL, registry and boot files — the only correct way to back up a DC. Restore a deleted object from the AD Recycle Bin first; restore a DC from system state and perform an <em>authoritative restore</em> with <code>ntdsutil</code> in Directory Services Restore Mode only when the Recycle Bin cannot help; never roll a DC back from a VM snapshot. Clients: Windows Backup to OneDrive (now an MD-102 objective), File History, Previous Versions, Reset this PC and the Recovery Environment. Define RPO and RTO in numbers — "lose at most 4 hours, be back in 8" — and test a restore every quarter; an untested backup is a hope.</p>
<h4>Performance troubleshooting</h4>
<p>Method first: define the symptom, measure, compare with a baseline, isolate, change one thing, verify. Tools: Task Manager, Resource Monitor (per-process disk and network), Performance Monitor counters and Data Collector Sets, Reliability Monitor, Windows Performance Recorder and Analyzer, DTrace on Server 2025, and Process Explorer. Thresholds experience has settled: Processor % Processor Time above 80% sustained is CPU-bound; Memory Available MBytes below 10% of RAM or high Pages/sec is memory pressure; PhysicalDisk Avg. Disk sec/Transfer above 20 ms is slow storage and Avg. Disk Queue Length above 2 per spindle is a queue; Network Bytes Total/sec near link speed is saturation; Private Bytes climbing for days is a leak. <code>Get-Counter '\\PhysicalDisk(_Total)\\Avg. Disk sec/Transfer' -SampleInterval 5 -MaxSamples 12</code> gives a minute of truth; <code>sfc /scannow</code> and <code>DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth</code> repair the OS image.</p>
<h4>On the exams</h4>
<p>MD-102 "Protect devices" (15–20%) is antivirus, disk encryption, firewall, ASR, Defender for Endpoint and update rings; "Manage and maintain devices" includes Windows Backup, remote actions and diagnostics. AZ-802 "Secure Windows Server infrastructure" (10–15%) and "Monitor and troubleshoot" (15–20%) cover auditing, performance and recovery. Both reward knowing the default numbers above.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>"The file server is slow at 9 a.m."</strong> Baseline from last month: Avg. Disk sec/Transfer 6 ms, queue 0.4. Today at 09:05: 48 ms, queue 7, Processor 22%, Available MBytes 11,000 of 16,384 — disk-bound, not CPU or memory. Resource Monitor's Disk tab shows <code>MsMpEng.exe</code> (Defender) and <code>vssvc.exe</code> dominating I/O; Task Scheduler shows a full Defender scan at 09:00 and a shadow copy at 09:00. Two scheduled jobs collide with the morning logon storm. Fix: move the full scan to 02:00 and shadow copies to 07:00 and 12:00, exclude the shadow-copy storage from real-time scanning per Microsoft's guidance, and set an alert when Avg. Disk sec/Transfer exceeds 20 ms for five minutes. Verify next morning: 7 ms, queue 0.5. Document the baseline in the runbook.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Antivirus is on, so the endpoint is protected.\" AV is one layer; ASR rules, Credential Guard, LSA protection, App Control, firewall scoping and EDR each stop classes of attack AV does not.","\"WSUS is dead.\" It is deprecated — still shipped in Server 2025 and still receiving security fixes, but gaining no features; plan a move to Windows Update for Business, Autopatch or Azure Update Manager.","\"A VM snapshot of the domain controller is a backup.\" Rolling back causes USN rollback and replication divergence; back up system state instead."],
problems:[
{q:"A DC logs 120 events/second averaging 700 bytes. How much per day, and how long does a 192 MB Security log last?", a:"120 × 700 = 84 KB/s ≈ 7.3 GB/day; 192 MB ÷ 84 KB/s ≈ 2,340 s ≈ 39 minutes. Forward events off-box and set the log to several GB."},
{q:"You see 400 events 4625 with logon type 3 in ten minutes, each for a different username, all from 10.10.20.77, with no 4740 lockouts. Diagnose and act.", a:"A password-spraying pattern (one attempt per account stays under the lockout threshold). Isolate 10.10.20.77, identify the process via 4688/Sysmon, check 4624 successes from that host, force resets for any success, and add an alert on failed-logon fan-out from one source."},
{q:"Design update rings for 500 devices with a 7-day quality deferral maximum and justify the sizes.", a:"Pilot: IT + volunteers, 1% (5 devices), 0-day deferral, deadline 2 days; Early: 10% (50 devices) across departments, 2-day deferral; Broad: the rest, 7-day deferral, 3-day deadline + 2-day grace. Pilot catches breakage before 10% see it; the deadline guarantees completion before next Patch Tuesday."},
{q:"A BitLocker-protected laptop asks for its recovery key after a firmware update. Why, how do you get the key, and how do you prevent it next time?", a:"The TPM measurements changed so the key would not unseal; retrieve the 48-digit key from Entra ID/Intune (or AD) for that device's key ID; next time run Suspend-BitLocker -RebootCount 1 before the firmware update."},
{q:"A file server shows Avg. Disk sec/Transfer 35 ms, queue 5, CPU 15%, plenty of free memory. Name the bottleneck, two likely causes and two remedies.", a:"Storage latency. Causes: contention from scans/backups/shadow copies, or undersized spinning disks. Remedies: reschedule competing jobs; move to SSD or add spindles/tiered Storage Spaces; verify after the change."}
],
checks:[{q:"Default Security log size, and the event ID for a failed logon?",a:"20 MB; 4625."},{q:"BitLocker's default cipher and recovery password length?",a:"XTS-AES 128; 48 digits."},{q:"Hotpatch baseline (reboot) months and the on-premises price?",a:"January, April, July, October; $1.50 per core per month via Azure Arc (free on Azure Edition)."}],
explain:"Give ADA a hardening and operations runbook for one Windows 11 laptop and one domain controller: the audit policy and log sizing, Defender and platform protections, BitLocker and LAPS, firewall scoping, the update ring, the backup method, and the five performance counters you check first."},

{id:"p16m7", title:"Windows Security Through the Attacker's Map, Defensively: Tiering, AD Weaknesses, Sysmon and Detection", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Explain why Active Directory is the pentester's and the defender's central map, and the legal frame around testing it.","Apply the tiered administration model and Microsoft's enterprise access model.","Describe the common Active Directory weaknesses conceptually and the fix for each.","Deploy Sysmon with a community configuration and read its key events.","Design detections from Windows and Sysmon events and route them to a SIEM and an incident process."],
body:`<p>Why does nearly every real intrusion report end in Active Directory? Because the domain is the one system that reaches every other: whoever controls a <strong>domain controller</strong> controls every workstation, server and credential in the forest. Attackers therefore treat AD as a map — a graph of accounts, groups, sessions and permissions — and search it for the shortest path from a phished user to Domain Admins. The best defenders read the same map first. This module teaches it from the defensive seat only: no attack procedures, every technique framed as a weakness to find and remove in your own domain, and practice confined to your lab or to sanctioned platforms (TryHackMe and Hack The Box AD tracks, or a deliberately vulnerable lab such as GOAD deployed on your own hypervisor). The legal line from Domain 8 is absolute: the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act makes unauthorized access a crime; you test only what you own or have written authorization and scope to test.</p>
<h4>The tiered administration model</h4>
<p>Microsoft's tier model sorts every asset by what its compromise would cost. <strong>Tier 0</strong> is the identity control plane: domain controllers, the AD database and its backups, PKI (AD CS), the Entra Connect server, the hypervisors and storage that host DCs, privileged access workstations (PAWs) and the accounts that administer them. Tier 1 is servers and applications; Tier 2 is workstations, users and the help desk. The one rule: <em>credentials never flow down</em>. A Tier 0 administrator never signs in to a Tier 1 or Tier 2 machine, because an interactive logon leaves credential material in LSASS where a compromised machine can harvest it. Enforce it with separate accounts per tier (<code>ana</code>, <code>ana-t1</code>, <code>ana-t0</code>), Group Policy logon restrictions ("Deny log on locally" and "Deny log on through Remote Desktop" for higher-tier accounts on lower-tier OUs), and a dedicated PAW for Tier 0 work. Microsoft's current <em>enterprise access model</em> extends the idea to the cloud with control, management and data planes. Supporting controls: the <em>Protected Users</em> group (no NTLM, no RC4, no delegation, no cached credentials, a 4-hour TGT), authentication policies and silos that bind Tier 0 accounts to Tier 0 machines, gMSA for services, just-in-time elevation through PIM, and the clean-source principle: anything that manages a Tier 0 system is itself Tier 0.</p>
<h4>Common weaknesses, conceptually, and the fix for each</h4>
<table><tr><th>Weakness</th><th>Why it hurts</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
<tr><td>Over-privileged accounts: dozens of Domain Admins, service accounts in admin groups, admins browsing the web as admins</td><td>Each is a path to Tier 0; one phish ends the domain</td><td>Tiering, empty Domain Admins except break-glass, PIM, quarterly review with Get-ADGroupMember -Recursive</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shared local Administrator password</td><td>One workstation compromise unlocks all of them</td><td>Windows LAPS everywhere</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legacy protocols: NTLMv1, LLMNR and NetBIOS name resolution, SMBv1, unsigned SMB and LDAP</td><td>Spoofable names and relayable authentication let a device on the LAN impersonate servers and users</td><td>LmCompatibilityLevel 5; disable LLMNR/NBT-NS; SMB signing (default in 2025) and LDAP signing with channel binding; NTLM auditing then blocking</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kerberos hygiene: service accounts with human passwords and SPNs, RC4 allowed, accounts without pre-authentication, unconstrained delegation, a krbtgt key never rotated</td><td>Weakly keyed tickets can be attacked offline; delegation lets a compromised server impersonate anyone; an old krbtgt key keeps forged tickets valid</td><td>gMSA/dMSA or 25+ character random passwords; AES only; pre-auth everywhere; constrained or resource-based delegation; "sensitive, cannot be delegated" for Tier 0; rotate krbtgt twice yearly</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Password spraying</strong> exposure: short passwords, no banned list, no MFA</td><td>One common password tried slowly against every account stays under lockout thresholds and usually finds someone</td><td>14+ character passphrases, Entra Password Protection on DCs, MFA, fine-grained password policies for admins, alerting on failed-logon fan-out</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dangerous ACLs, AD CS templates and hygiene: broad groups with GenericAll or WriteDACL on OUs, groups or GPOs; certificate templates any user can enroll for any identity; Print Spooler on DCs; credentials in SYSVOL scripts; ntds.dit backups on file shares</td><td>Permissions, not exploits, become the path, and graph tools find them in minutes</td><td>Audit with PingCastle or Purple Knight and read BloodHound Community Edition output as a defender; restrict templates; disable the spooler on DCs; scrub SYSVOL; treat DC backups as Tier 0</td></tr></table>
<h4>Credential theft is the engine</h4>
<p>Almost every path across the map runs through <strong>LSASS</strong>, which keeps credential material in memory for single sign-on on any machine where an administrator has logged on. The attacker pattern, stated only as a shape: a foothold on one workstation, local administrator, harvest what LSASS holds, use it on the next machine, repeat until a Tier 0 credential appears. Every control here cuts one link: LAPS removes the shared local admin; tiering keeps Tier 0 credentials out of workstation memory; <em>Credential Guard</em> and LSA protection make LSASS memory unreadable to ordinary code; Protected Users and Remote Credential Guard stop credentials landing on the far machine; firewall scoping blocks workstation-to-workstation SMB and WinRM; MFA and passkeys make stolen passwords insufficient. Detection follows the same shape: an administrator account producing 4624 type 3 logons across many workstations, 4672 where no admin should appear, 4648 explicit-credential logons from a user workstation, and <strong>Sysmon</strong> event 10 showing an unexpected process opening <code>lsass.exe</code>.</p>
<h4>Sysmon and detection engineering</h4>
<p>Sysmon (System Monitor, part of Sysinternals, v15.22 as of September 2026) is a driver plus service that writes rich events to Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational. Install with a configuration — <code>sysmon64 -accepteula -i sysmonconfig.xml</code>, update with <code>-c</code> — and start from SwiftOnSecurity's sysmon-config or Olaf Hartong's sysmon-modular, which exclude the noise and tag events with MITRE ATT&amp;CK technique IDs. Events that carry detections: 1 (process creation with command line, hashes and parent), 3 (network connection by process), 7 (image loaded), 8 (CreateRemoteThread), 10 (ProcessAccess, including LSASS), 11 (file created), 12/13 (registry), 17/18 (named pipes), 22 (DNS query by process), 23/26 (file deleted), 25 (process tampering), 27–29 (executables blocked or detected). Size the log at a gigabyte or more and forward it. Then write detections as questions the logs can answer:</p>
<table><tr><th>Question</th><th>Signal</th></tr>
<tr><td>Did Office spawn a shell?</td><td>Sysmon 1: ParentImage WINWORD/EXCEL/OUTLOOK, Image powershell, cmd, wscript or mshta</td></tr>
<tr><td>Is PowerShell being used offensively?</td><td>4104 script blocks with encoded or download-and-execute content; Sysmon 1 with -EncodedCommand</td></tr>
<tr><td>Is someone spraying passwords?</td><td>4625 or 4771 for many distinct accounts from one source in minutes, few or no 4740</td></tr>
<tr><td>Are service tickets being harvested?</td><td>Bursts of 4769 with etype 0x17 (RC4) for many SPNs from one account</td></tr>
<tr><td>Is a non-DC replicating the directory?</td><td>4662 on a DC with replication control-access rights from an account that is not a DC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Persistence or track-covering?</td><td>7045, 4698, Sysmon 12/13 on Run keys across many hosts; 1102 audit log cleared; 4768 for a honeytoken account nobody should use</td></tr></table>
<p>The pipeline: Sysmon and audit policy → Windows Event Forwarding or an agent → a SIEM (Wazuh, Elastic, Microsoft Sentinel) → Sigma-style rules → an alert → the incident-response process from Domain 7. Microsoft Defender for Identity adds a sensor on each DC that detects many of these patterns out of the box. Tune for your environment: a detection that fires on every logon is deleted within a week.</p>
<h4>Hardening checklist for your lab domain</h4>
<ul><li>Separate accounts per tier, Tier 0 admins in Protected Users, logon restrictions by Group Policy, a PAW for Tier 0.</li><li>Windows LAPS on every machine; gMSA for every service; krbtgt rotated.</li><li>SMB and LDAP signing, LLMNR and NetBIOS off, LmCompatibilityLevel 5, NTLM audited; AES-only Kerberos; no unconstrained delegation; pre-auth required.</li><li>Print Spooler disabled on DCs; SYSVOL free of secrets; DC backups treated as Tier 0.</li><li>Credential Guard, LSA protection, ASR rules, Defender with Tamper Protection, host firewall scoped.</li><li>Advanced audit policy with command-line logging, Sysmon with a community configuration, logs forwarded off-box; MFA and passkeys for every administrator; a quarterly PingCastle or Purple Knight scan with a written remediation list.</li></ul>
<h4>On the exams and your career</h4>
<p>AZ-802's "Secure AD DS" objective and MD-102's Defender for Endpoint and attack surface reduction content live here. Beyond the exams, the certifications Domain 8 names are overwhelmingly about Active Directory: understanding the map as a defender is what lets you walk it legitimately as a tester, and it is what makes a pentest report useful to the administrators who read it.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A detection, start to finish, in your own lab.</strong> 10:14:02 — Sysmon 1 on LAP-07: ParentImage <code>WINWORD.EXE</code>, Image <code>powershell.exe</code>, CommandLine containing <code>-nop -w hidden -enc</code>. 10:14:03 — PowerShell 4104 records the decoded script block: a download from an unknown host and in-memory execution. 10:14:05 — Sysmon 3: powershell.exe connects to 203.0.113.40:443; Sysmon 22 shows the DNS query that preceded it. 10:16 — Sysmon 10: a new process opens <code>lsass.exe</code> with a read access mask (blocked by LSA protection, but logged). 10:19 — 4624 type 3 on FS01 from LAP-07 with the account <code>ana-t1</code>, which has never logged on from a workstation. The SIEM correlates the five events into one incident with dwell time under six minutes. Response (Domain 7): isolate LAP-07 through Defender for Endpoint, disable and reset <code>ana-t1</code> and the user's account, hunt 4624/4672 for that account on every server, pull the PowerShell transcript, submit the sample for Domain 11 analysis, and write the lesson: an ASR rule blocking Office child processes would have stopped step one. In your lab, generate the benign version — a scheduled task starting PowerShell from a Word macro you wrote — and watch the same five events appear.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Using Domain Admin for everything in my lab is harmless.\" Habits transfer; practicing tiering in the lab is the only way it becomes reflex in production.","\"Sysmon will slow down the machine.\" With a community configuration it costs a few percent CPU at most; the log volume, not the CPU, is what you must size.","\"Our DCs are fully patched, so AD is secure.\" Most AD compromises use misconfiguration and credential theft, not unpatched CVEs; the weakness table is the real attack surface."],
problems:[
{q:"Classify into tiers: (a) the Entra Connect server, (b) the file server, (c) the help-desk technician's laptop, (d) the Hyper-V host running a DC, (e) the backup server holding DC system-state backups.", a:"(a) Tier 0, (b) Tier 1, (c) Tier 2, (d) Tier 0 (hosts Tier 0), (e) Tier 0 (holds the AD database)."},
{q:"Lockout policy: 10 failures per 15 minutes; 500 user accounts. Explain why one password tried against every account every 20 minutes never locks anyone out, and name the log pattern and two controls that defeat it.", a:"Each account sees one failure per 20 minutes, far under 10 per 15, so 4740 never fires. Pattern: 4625/4771 fan-out — many distinct accounts, one source, short window. Controls: MFA and banned-password lists (plus alerting on the fan-out)."},
{q:"Write the detection logic, in words, for 'Office spawned a shell' and name one false-positive source.", a:"Sysmon 1 where ParentImage ends in WINWORD.EXE, EXCEL.EXE, POWERPNT.EXE or OUTLOOK.EXE and Image ends in powershell.exe, pwsh.exe, cmd.exe, wscript.exe, cscript.exe or mshta.exe. False positive: an add-in or legitimate macro that runs a script; whitelist by hash or command line."},
{q:"Plan a krbtgt key rotation for a two-site domain with a 180-minute site-link interval.", a:"Reset once, wait for full replication (at least one inter-site interval; verify with repadmin /showrepl), then reset again; 10-hour TGTs issued under the old key fail after the second reset, so do it at low-use hours and announce it. Repeat yearly and after any suspected compromise."},
{q:"A PingCastle report flags 23 members of Domain Admins, 4 accounts with 'Do not require Kerberos preauthentication', and unconstrained delegation on two member servers. Order the remediation and justify.", a:"(1) Remove unconstrained delegation (any user touching those servers can be impersonated, including admins), (2) fix pre-auth flags (offline attack exposure), (3) cut Domain Admins to break-glass plus a tiered admin set with PIM — the largest change, so it needs planning but is the biggest long-term win."}
],
checks:[{q:"Which tier holds domain controllers and the servers that back them up?",a:"Tier 0."},{q:"Sysmon event IDs for process creation, network connection and LSASS access?",a:"1, 3 and 10."},{q:"Where do Sysmon events land?",a:"Applications and Services Logs → Microsoft → Windows → Sysmon → Operational."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA the tiered model with the 'credentials never flow down' rule, then walk through three weaknesses from the table — why each is a path to Tier 0 and the exact control that removes it — and finish with the five log events you would alert on first."}
],
listening:[],
labs:[
{id:"p16lab1", title:"Build the domain: Server Core DC, DNS, DHCP and a joined client", goal:"A working Active Directory lab on your own hypervisor.", steps:["Download the Windows Server 2025 evaluation ISO (180 days) and the Windows 11 Enterprise evaluation; create a private virtual switch in Hyper-V, VirtualBox or Proxmox (Generation 2 / UEFI, vTPM for the client).","Install DC01 as Server Core (2 vCPU, 4 GB): sconfig for name and static IP, then Install-WindowsFeature AD-Domain-Services, DNS, DHCP and Install-ADDSForest for corp.&lt;a domain you own&gt;. Authorize DHCP and create a scope with exclusions and options 003/006/015.","Verify: dcdiag, nslookup -type=SRV _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.&lt;domain&gt;, Get-ADDomain, netdom query fsmo.","Join the Windows 11 client, log on as a domain user, and capture the 4768/4769 events on DC01 for that logon."], deliverable:"Screenshots of dcdiag, the SRV lookup and the Kerberos events, plus a one-page network diagram with IPs, roles and the DHCP scope."},
{id:"p16lab2", title:"OUs, groups and Group Policy with LAPS", goal:"A delegated, policy-managed domain you can explain.", steps:["Create the OU structure from Module 4 (Tier0, Servers, Workstations, Users, Groups, ServiceAccounts, Staging) and AGDLP groups for two file shares on a member server FS01.","Create three GPOs: Baseline-Workstations (screen lock, firewall on, PowerShell Script Block Logging), Baseline-Servers, and Tier0-Restrictions (deny interactive logon of Tier 0 accounts on Workstations). Test precedence with one Enforced link and one Block Inheritance.","Run Update-LapsADSchema, enable Windows LAPS by GPO for Workstations, force a rotation and retrieve the password with Get-LapsADPassword.","Delegate Reset Password on OU=Users to a help-desk group and prove it works and that it fails on OU=Tier0."], deliverable:"gpresult /h for a workstation and a Tier 0 PAW, the LAPS retrieval screenshot, and a paragraph explaining each precedence outcome."},
{id:"p16lab3", title:"PowerShell audit toolkit with remoting", goal:"Reusable scripts that audit machines the way an assessor would.", steps:["Write Get-LocalAccountReport (local users, Administrators members, password age) and Get-ServiceRisk (Automatic-but-stopped services, services running from user-writable paths, unquoted service paths) as functions in a module with a manifest.","Run them against DC01, FS01 and the client with Invoke-Command, collect results with PSComputerName, export to CSV, and handle an unreachable host without aborting the run.","Add a Pester test for one function and run PSScriptAnalyzer on the module.","Register the audit as a scheduled task running as a gMSA (New-ADServiceAccount, Install-ADServiceAccount) and confirm event 4698 recorded it."], deliverable:"The module, the CSV output, the Pester and analyzer results, and the task definition."},
{id:"p16lab4", title:"Sysmon, audit policy and a detection", goal:"Logs that answer questions, on every lab machine.", steps:["Enable Advanced Audit Policy by GPO: logon events, account management, process creation with command line, directory service access on DC01; raise the Security log to 1 GB.","Install Sysmon with sysmon-modular or SwiftOnSecurity's configuration on all machines; confirm events 1, 3 and 22 appear in Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational.","Configure Windows Event Forwarding to a collector (or deploy a free Wazuh server) and confirm the DC's 4769 and the client's Sysmon 1 arrive.","Generate a benign test: a Word document whose macro starts PowerShell with a harmless command; find the parent–child event, the 4104 script block and the network event, and write the detection rule in words."], deliverable:"The audit policy export (auditpol /backup), the Sysmon config you used, screenshots of the forwarded events, and your written detection."},
{id:"p16lab5", title:"Cloud endpoint management with an Intune trial", goal:"Entra join, enrollment, compliance and Conditional Access on a device you own.", steps:["Start the free 30-day Intune trial tenant; enable automatic MDM enrollment and create two break-glass accounts.","Entra-join a fresh Windows 11 VM during OOBE with a trial user; confirm enrollment in Intune and run Sync.","Create a compliance policy (BitLocker, TPM, Defender, OS version), a disk-encryption endpoint security policy and a LAPS policy; watch the device go compliant.","Create a Conditional Access policy requiring MFA and a compliant device, in report-only mode first, then enabled; register Windows Hello for Business; import an Autopilot hardware hash from the VM and build a user-driven profile."], deliverable:"Screenshots of the compliance report, the Conditional Access sign-in log entry, the Autopilot device list, and a half-page on what you would change for 500 devices."}
],
capstone:{title:"A hardened small-business domain, documented like a consultant", brief:"Design, build and document a complete Windows environment for a 25-user company on hardware you own: two Server Core domain controllers with DNS and DHCP, a member file server with DFS and shadow copies, a Windows 11 client, a tiered OU and group design with AGDLP, baseline and Tier 0 GPOs, Windows LAPS, gMSA-run services, Sysmon and advanced auditing forwarded to a collector or SIEM, a PowerShell audit module on a schedule, a backup and restore test of a domain controller's system state, and an Intune trial tenant with compliance and Conditional Access policies for the client. Deliver a runbook with a network diagram, the licensing and identity cost arithmetic for the real company, and a one-page risk register listing the weaknesses from Module 7 that you verified are absent.", rubric:["Domain works end to end: dcdiag clean, replication healthy, Kerberos-only authentication demonstrated (4769 AES, no 4776).","Tiering is real: separate accounts, Protected Users, logon restrictions enforced and tested; LAPS and gMSA in use.","Detection works: Sysmon plus audit policy forwarded off-box with at least three written detections tested against benign triggers.","Recovery works: a system-state backup restored (or an object restored from the Recycle Bin) with timings recorded.","Documentation is complete: diagram, licensing arithmetic (cores, packs, CALs, Entra/Intune licenses), runbook and risk register that ADA could follow."]},
exam:[
{id:"p16q1", type:"mc", q:"Which file contains both the Windows kernel and the executive?", options:["hal.dll","ntoskrnl.exe","ntdll.dll","win32k.sys"], answer:1, why:"ntoskrnl.exe holds the kernel and the executive; ntdll.dll is the user-mode system-call gateway; win32k.sys is the windowing driver."},
{id:"p16q2", type:"mc", q:"A process named svchost.exe runs from C:\\Users\\Public with parent explorer.exe. The best conclusion:", options:["Normal: svchost can run anywhere","Masquerading: real svchost lives in System32 under services.exe","A Windows update in progress","A Hyper-V worker process"], answer:1, why:"Path and parent are the two properties malware cannot fake cheaply; legitimate svchost.exe is in System32 and is started by services.exe."},
{id:"p16q3", type:"mc", q:"Where are the HKLM registry hive files stored on disk?", options:["C:\\Windows\\System32\\config","C:\\Windows\\SYSVOL","C:\\ProgramData\\Registry","C:\\Windows\\NTDS"], answer:0, why:"SAM, SECURITY, SOFTWARE, SYSTEM and DEFAULT live in System32\\config; NTDS holds the AD database; SYSVOL holds Group Policy templates."},
{id:"p16q4", type:"mc", q:"Why must Format-Table be the last cmdlet in a pipeline?", options:["It is slow","It emits formatting objects other cmdlets cannot process","It requires administrator rights","It closes the remoting session"], answer:1, why:"Format-* cmdlets produce formatting instructions for the host, not the original objects."},
{id:"p16q5", type:"mc", q:"Default ports for PowerShell remoting over WinRM:", options:["22 and 23","135 and 445","5985 (HTTP) and 5986 (HTTPS)","3389 and 3390"], answer:2, why:"WS-Management uses 5985 for HTTP and 5986 for HTTPS; in a domain, authentication is Kerberos."},
{id:"p16q6", type:"mc", q:"Which PowerShell event ID records de-obfuscated script blocks?", options:["4624","4688","4104","7045"], answer:2, why:"Event 4104 in Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational is Script Block Logging; 4688 is process creation; 7045 is a new service."},
{id:"p16q7", type:"mc", q:"A host has two 16-core processors and will run 4 VMs. Minimum Windows Server Standard core licenses?", options:["16","32","64","128"], answer:2, why:"32 cores per full set; Standard allows 2 VMs per set; 4 VMs need 2 sets = 64 core licenses (four 16-core packs)."},
{id:"p16q8", type:"mc", q:"Which installation option has no local GUI, cannot be changed after install, and is recommended for domain controllers?", options:["Desktop Experience","Server Core","Nano Server","Essentials"], answer:1, why:"Server Core is managed remotely (PowerShell, RSAT, Windows Admin Center); since Server 2016 the option is fixed at install."},
{id:"p16q9", type:"mc", q:"A Windows DHCP server on a domain network hands out no leases although its scope is active. Most likely cause:", options:["Lease time too long","The server is not authorized in Active Directory","Option 015 is missing","DNS scavenging is off"], answer:1, why:"Domain-member DHCP servers must be authorized in AD; unauthorized servers stop serving."},
{id:"p16q10", type:"mc", q:"In Kerberos, the ticket-granting ticket is encrypted with:", options:["The user's password hash","The service account's key","The krbtgt account's key","The computer account's key"], answer:2, why:"Only domain controllers hold the krbtgt key, so only they can read and issue TGTs; service tickets use the service account's key."},
{id:"p16q11", type:"mc", q:"GPO A at the domain is Enforced (lock after 15 min); OU=Sales has Block Inheritance and GPO B (60 min). A Sales computer locks after:", options:["60 minutes","15 minutes","Neither applies","Whichever GPO was created first"], answer:1, why:"Enforced links override Block Inheritance and win conflicts with lower-level GPOs."},
{id:"p16q12", type:"mc", q:"Which FSMO role is the domain's authoritative time source and receives urgent password changes?", options:["Schema Master","RID Master","PDC Emulator","Infrastructure Master"], answer:2, why:"The PDC Emulator handles time, password-change forwarding, lockouts and default GPO editing."},
{id:"p16q13", type:"mc", q:"Default inter-site Active Directory replication interval:", options:["15 seconds","15 minutes","180 minutes","24 hours"], answer:2, why:"Site links default to 180 minutes (minimum 15); intra-site replication starts 15 seconds after a change."},
{id:"p16q14", type:"mc", q:"Which license tier is the minimum for Microsoft Entra Conditional Access?", options:["Entra ID Free","Entra ID P1","Entra ID P2","Microsoft 365 F1"], answer:1, why:"Conditional Access and dynamic groups require P1; P2 adds Identity Protection and PIM; Free tenants get Security Defaults."},
{id:"p16q15", type:"mc", q:"Which Windows Autopilot mode suits shared lobby kiosks with no assigned user?", options:["User-driven","Pre-provisioning","Self-deploying","Hybrid join"], answer:2, why:"Self-deploying mode requires no user sign-in and needs TPM 2.0 attestation."},
{id:"p16q16", type:"mc", q:"Windows LAPS solves which problem?", options:["Slow Group Policy refresh","The same local Administrator password on every machine","Expired Kerberos tickets","Unsigned drivers"], answer:1, why:"LAPS gives each device a unique, rotating local admin password backed up to Entra ID or AD, cutting off lateral movement by shared credentials."},
{id:"p16q17", type:"sa", q:"You see 300 events 4625 (logon type 3) in eight minutes for 300 different usernames from one internal IP, and no 4740 lockouts. Name the pattern, explain why lockout did not trigger, and list three actions in order.", answer:"Password spraying: one attempt per account stays under the lockout threshold. Actions: isolate the source host; identify the process (4688/Sysmon 1) and any 4624 successes from that source and force resets on them; add an alert for failed-logon fan-out and deploy MFA and banned-password lists.", rubric:["Names spraying and the per-account-threshold reason","Isolation and success-hunting","Preventive controls (alerting, MFA, banned passwords)"]},
{id:"p16q18", type:"sa", q:"Explain the tiered administration model to a new administrator: what Tier 0 contains, the one rule, and two controls that enforce the rule.", answer:"Tier 0 is the identity control plane: DCs, the AD database and its backups, PKI, Entra Connect, hypervisors hosting DCs, PAWs and their admin accounts. Rule: credentials never flow down — Tier 0 accounts never log on to Tier 1/2 machines. Controls: separate accounts per tier with Group Policy logon restrictions (deny log on locally/RDP), Protected Users and authentication policy silos, and a dedicated PAW.", rubric:["Correct Tier 0 contents","States 'credentials never flow down'","Two concrete enforcing controls"]}
],
cards:[
{id:"p16c1", front:"Kernel mode", back:"Ring 0: the kernel, executive and drivers share one address space; a driver bug crashes the whole system. User-mode code (ring 3) reaches it only through ntdll.dll's system calls."},
{id:"p16c2", front:"Access token", back:"The object attached to a process that says who it is: user SID, group SIDs, privileges and integrity level. UAC gives administrators a filtered Medium token and a full High token."},
{id:"p16c3", front:"Registry hive", back:"A registry file on disk: SAM, SECURITY, SOFTWARE, SYSTEM, DEFAULT in C:\\Windows\\System32\\config and NTUSER.DAT per profile; loaded under HKLM and HKU."},
{id:"p16c4", front:"NTFS", back:"Windows' journaling file system: Master File Table, 4 KB clusters, alternate data streams, links, shadow copies, and security descriptors with DACL (explicit beats inherited; Deny beats Allow) and SACL."},
{id:"p16c5", front:"Sysinternals", back:"Russinovich's free toolset: Process Explorer (what runs, signed?), Autoruns (how it comes back), Procmon (what it touches), Sysmon (what to log), plus TCPView, Sigcheck, AccessChk, ProcDump."},
{id:"p16c6", front:"Pipeline", back:"PowerShell passes .NET objects, not text, from cmdlet to cmdlet; Where-Object filters, Select-Object projects, Sort/Group/Measure-Object shape; Format-* goes last."},
{id:"p16c7", front:"Cmdlet", back:"A PowerShell command named Verb-Noun from about 100 approved verbs; discover with Get-Command, read with Get-Help, inspect output with Get-Member."},
{id:"p16c8", front:"WinRM", back:"Windows Remote Management (WS-Management) over HTTP 5985 / HTTPS 5986; carries Enter-PSSession and Invoke-Command; Kerberos in a domain, TrustedHosts or HTTPS in a workgroup; JEA constrains endpoints."},
{id:"p16c9", front:"Server Core", back:"Windows Server without a local GUI: smaller attack surface and fewer reboots; managed by PowerShell, RSAT and Windows Admin Center; the option is fixed at install; the choice for DCs and Hyper-V hosts."},
{id:"p16c10", front:"Hyper-V", back:"Microsoft's type-1 hypervisor: Generation 2 VMs (UEFI, Secure Boot, vTPM), VHDX to 64 TB, production vs standard checkpoints, external/internal/private switches, nested virtualization; GPU partitioning in 2025."},
{id:"p16c11", front:"Domain controller", back:"A server holding ntds.dit and running the KDC, LDAP and (usually) DNS and Global Catalog; located by clients through DNS SRV records; backed up only via system state."},
{id:"p16c12", front:"Kerberos", back:"Ticket authentication on port 88: AS-REQ/REP gives a TGT (krbtgt key, 10 h); TGS-REQ/REP gives a service ticket (service key, PAC with SIDs); AP-REQ presents it. Needs DNS, SPNs and clocks within 5 minutes; AES, not RC4."},
{id:"p16c13", front:"Group Policy", back:"GPOs (AD container + SYSVOL template) linked to site, domain and OU; processed Local→Site→Domain→OU, last wins; Enforced beats Block Inheritance; refresh 90 min + 0–30 random (DCs 5 min)."},
{id:"p16c14", front:"FSMO roles", back:"Five single-master roles: Schema Master and Domain Naming Master (forest); RID Master (500-RID pools), PDC Emulator (time, passwords, lockouts) and Infrastructure Master (domain). Transfer gracefully; seize only when the holder is gone."},
{id:"p16c15", front:"Conditional Access", back:"Entra ID's if-then policy engine (P1): signals (user, app, device state, location, risk) → grant with MFA/compliant device or block; build in report-only, exclude break-glass accounts."},
{id:"p16c16", front:"Windows Autopilot", back:"Cloud provisioning from the factory: hardware hash → profile → OOBE joins Entra ID and enrolls in Intune. Modes: user-driven, pre-provisioning, self-deploying (TPM 2.0); device preparation (v2) vs deployment profiles."},
{id:"p16c17", front:"BitLocker", back:"Full-volume encryption, XTS-AES 128 default, key sealed to TPM 2.0 (+PIN optional), 48-digit recovery password escrowed to Entra ID/AD; protects data at rest only; on by default in Windows 11 24H2 clean installs."},
{id:"p16c18", front:"Windows LAPS", back:"Built-in local administrator password rotation (default 30 days) with backup to Entra ID or AD (Update-LapsADSchema); 2025 adds managed accounts and passphrases; kills the shared-local-admin lateral-movement path."}
]
});
